Revision history for Tira

3.89  2026-08-24

    - TKT-493. Requested directly: "create a new modal on the html
      dashboard, the user can view and edit and add the policies not
      just column policies." The Columns dialog only ever covered a
      column's own required-action template; the board-wide police
      policy engine (36 rules - card-duration, wip-limit,
      conversation-not-folded, and the rest) was CLI-only, reachable
      through tira.policy.add/list/remove/decline and nowhere in the
      browser.

    - New: a Policies button next to Columns opens a modal listing
      every declared, declined, and undeclared rule (the same
      three-way split tira.policy.review already returns), with a
      form to declare a new policy, edit or remove a declared one, or
      decline a rule with a reason - covering every rule-specific
      parameter tira.policy.add accepts (enter, before, column, age,
      read_age, max, pattern, message, require, sandbox, require_link,
      link_to) plus the type/on_column/ref scope narrowing, so nothing
      typeable on the CLI is unreachable from the dashboard.

    - New engine method policy_rule_specs exposes the same
      needs/forbids data policy_add already validates against, so the
      form's rule picker shows or hides the right fields per rule
      without duplicating that knowledge and risking drift.

    - New browser_providers closures (policies, policy_add,
      policy_remove, policy_decline) and matching DashboardWeb.pm
      routes (GET /policies, POST /policy/add, /policy/remove,
      /policy/decline), following the same provider/route pattern the
      Columns dialog already uses for /columns and /columns/apply.

    - Editing a declared policy removes and re-declares it under the
      hood, since policy_add itself refuses to update a declaration
      in place (TKT-339's own rule against silently replacing a
      stricter policy with a looser one) - the modal keeps that
      guarantee rather than working around it.

    - TKT-494. "Add a UI to allow to select which column is new
      ticket entry point. It can be multiple entries" (owner, live).
      The Columns dialog gains an Entry checkbox per column row,
      building on the multi-entry engine support TKT-496 shipped
      alongside it. Saving sends the whole checked set through
      column_roles_set in one call - full-replace semantics matching
      repeating --role entry=X on the command line, not an add-only
      merge, so unchecking a column and saving removes it rather than
      only ever growing the set. A save that never touches the
      checkboxes sends no entry field and leaves the declared entry
      columns untouched.

    - TKT-284. column-skipped's only remedy on finished work used to
      be walking the card backward and forward through the column it
      missed, writing moves into its history that never happened -
      the same falsified-history shape 2.35 already fixed for
      orphan-card, unfixed here. Reported at 4x the count: 344
      outstanding findings against 439 done cards on one board,
      mostly work that finished before the rule existed. Any comment
      on the card now settles the finding, reusing
      discard-unexplained's own settle check rather than inventing a
      second convention - the comment is the acknowledgement, and
      nothing is rewritten. A card with the same shortcut and no
      comment is still reported, exactly as before.

    - TKT-292. tira.usage documented no allowed values for
      --sdlc-gate, and gate.add's --gate had none either - both took
      free text, so no typo was ever refused and gate-missing could
      neither be satisfied nor caught being satisfied falsely.

    - New: tira.project.gates declares a project's own gate
      vocabulary (--gate-name, repeatable), opt-in the same way
      column_roles already is - unrestricted, exactly today's
      behaviour, until a project declares one. Once declared, --gate
      and --sdlc-gate are both validated against that one list,
      refusing a name outside it and naming the declared vocabulary.
      record_update's sdlc_gate handling, previously bundled into a
      generic loop that applied zero validation, is carved out into
      its own validated assignment; create_record gets the same check
      at creation time.

    - TKT-303. "Both halves are correct about the DECLARED repo and
      wrong about the work" - card-sandbox-missing assumes one project
      maps to one repository, and a card whose work legitimately lives
      in a second one had no honest way to clear the finding but
      declining the whole rule (losing the check for every other
      card) or a permanent violation nobody could act on.

    - New: tira.policy.decline takes an optional --ref CARD, scoping
      the decision to one card rather than the whole board - a
      different question than board-wide decline ("should this rule
      exist"), so it does not conflict with the rule staying declared
      everywhere else. Requires a reason exactly like board-wide
      decline, reads back through tira.policy.declined --ref CARD, and
      is reusable by any rule, not scoped to card-sandbox-missing.

    - TKT-332. "Four epic agents were spawned... All four cards had
      agent_session: null... ALL FOUR failed with 'No agent named ...
      is reachable'." None of the 35 existing policy rules asked
      whether a card being worked had an agent_session recorded, even
      though record_update updates 'sandbox agent_session' in the same
      qw() list, one word apart, and card-sandbox-missing exists for
      the first.

    - New policy rule: card-agentless (--enter COLUMN), same shape as
      card-sandbox-missing, reporting a card in the working column
      with no agent_session recorded. A different question from
      card-unassigned - assignee says who owns the work, agent_session
      says whether the worker can be reached again - so a card can be
      correctly assigned while permanently orphaned from its agent,
      and the two rules compose rather than merge. Settles the moment
      the handle is written down.

    - TKT-357. "ZEPG-6 has a checklist of ELEVEN ITEMS, ALL DONE.
      checklist-idle fired on it at 3h and escalated to CRITICAL.
      There is nothing left to tick." The rule was already correct -
      it fires on a complete checklist and moving the card settles it
      - but "no checklist movement since TIMESTAMP" named the one
      action that cannot be taken and never the one that works.

    - Fixed: checklist-idle's message now distinguishes a 100%-
      complete checklist ("move the card, there is nothing left to
      tick") from one with unticked items (unchanged wording), reusing
      checklist-unmoved's own case-insensitive all-done check. No
      behaviour change - when the rule fires and what settles it are
      both unaffected.

    - TKT-399. t/239, whose whole job is proving every declared
      refusal is exercised, counted its own parse against the
      declaration for one table and not the other - "a guard written
      to prove every declared refusal is exercised, silently covering
      less than it claims, is the fault it exists to catch one level
      up," in the file's own words about the first table's history.
      Now asserts the same parsed-count-equals-declared-count for the
      second table too. No live loss today (3 declared, 3 found); the
      new assertion was proven a genuine guard by temporarily
      reproducing the described failure mode and confirming it fails.

    - TKT-406. discard-with-open-questions correctly fires for any
      ending column, not just discard - but its message still said
      "set aside carrying..." for a card that reached done, wording
      written for the discard case and shown on every ending since.
      Not a functional gap; the finding was always correct and named.

    - Fixed: the message now names the ending the card actually
      reached ("set aside" only in the discard column itself, "reached
      done" or whichever other column a board marks --terminal
      otherwise). "Discard them here", about the leftover questions
      rather than the card, is unchanged either way.

    - TKT-499. With two card-sandbox-missing policies declared for two
      acceptable sandboxes, one card produced two findings whose
      detail text was character-for-character identical - both fell
      back to the same generic wording once the card recorded no
      sandbox at all.

    - Fixed: the six machine-watching policy rules now dedupe on
      (rule, ref, detail) within one pass - two policies reporting the
      exact same fact about the exact same card report once, not
      twice. Two policies describing genuinely different problems (one
      satisfied, one not) still both report.

    - TKT-283. dev.found.bug_or_improvement --help named no arguments,
      so the one command an agent reaches for after already hitting
      trouble was the one it could not look up without a second
      command. SKILLS.md mentioned it only mid-sentence in prose, never
      at the start of a line, so _skills_usage_line()'s start-of-line
      lookup never matched it and --help fell through to the bare
      "[options]" placeholder - the same defect shape TKT-343 fixed for
      six other commands.

    - Fixed: SKILLS.md now also carries a start-of-line usage line for
      tira.dev.found.bug_or_improvement, naming --from, --title, and
      --text.

    - TKT-410. card-metrics --require scope_in,scope_out was permanently
      unsatisfiable: the check reads a card's fields with a raw
      $record->{$_} lookup, and scope_in/scope_out are never flat
      record fields on any type - only nested under
      scope.included/scope.excluded. Measured live on both a ticket
      and an epic with scope explicitly set via --scope-in/--scope-out
      - both still tripped the rule.

    - Fixed: card-metrics now special-cases scope_in and scope_out to
      read the nested scope object, the same lookup
      _policy_missing_detail already used, so the requirement is
      satisfiable on tickets and epics alike. Every other --require
      field keeps its existing flat lookup.

    - TKT-424. priority-skipped fired on a parent (sow/epic) sitting
      open only because a child is open under it, exactly as it fired
      on one genuinely being worked ahead of turn - 10 manual
      rule.suspend calls in one afternoon, all for the same bookkeeping
      reason. Q-074 asked which of two designs to use; the owner chose
      option B.

    - Fixed: priority-skipped now exempts a parent with no assignee AND
      at least one open child AND whose own last activity is no later
      than that child's own - an assigned parent, or one edited after
      its child without being formally claimed, still fires exactly as
      before.

3.88  2026-08-24

    - TKT-498. Owner, in Cantonese, watching an agent drop a field:
      "I want a command that shows immediately which parts are
      missing."

    - New: tira.ticket.missing / tira.epic.missing / tira.sow.missing
      --ref REF answers the same question card-full-details already
      computes internally to fire a violation - which of a complete
      card's fields are still empty - for the named card in any
      column, on demand, rather than waiting for the card to reach
      its declared entry column and for police to notice. A new
      record_missing engine method wraps the existing
      _card_missing_from helper and is reached through the same
      generic record.(show|list|update|move|discard|restore|clone)
      dispatch every other record command already uses, now with
      "missing" added to the alternation. t/377 proves a bare card
      reports every required field missing, a fully-filled/
      checklisted/parented card reports nothing missing, and all
      three record types answer through the shared dispatch - red
      against pre-fix code (the method does not exist), green after.
      Docker: Files=372, Tests=7543, PASS, 100% statement+subroutine
      coverage on lib/Tira.pm and lib/Tira/CLI.pm.

3.87  2026-08-24

    - TKT-491. Found during a documentation-gap hunt while checking
      for other instances of the exact pattern TKT-280/488/490
      already fixed: comment_update and comment_remove were the last
      two record-entry lookups with the identical gap.

    - Fixed: comment.update and comment.remove refused an unknown
      --comment id with only "Comment '<id>' not found" - true, and
      silent about the fact that ids are CMT-NNN rather than a
      position, the same gap already closed for checklist entries
      (TKT-280), required-action items (TKT-488), and gate/evidence
      entries (TKT-490). Both now name the card's real ids when it
      has any, or the CMT-NNN shape when it has none. t/376 proves
      both commands, both against a card with real comments and an
      empty one - red against pre-fix code (8 of 12 assertions
      failed), green after. Docker: Files=371, Tests=7517, PASS,
      100% statement+subroutine coverage on lib/Tira.pm.

3.86  2026-08-24

    - TKT-497. "When card being move along on the html dashboard. The
      population of the required action items will be duplicated."

    - Fixed: two near-simultaneous browser moves of the same card (a
      double-click on the status dropdown, or any other double-fire
      of the same request) could both read the card's required_items
      before either had written its own addition, both decide a
      column's required-action template item was missing, and both
      add it - leaving two identical REQ-NNN entries for the same
      text and column. required_item_add's auto-populate path
      (source eq 'required-action') now checks and adds atomically
      inside the project lock it already holds, so the second of two
      concurrent calls sees the first's addition and skips it. A
      manual tira.required-action.add is unaffected - only the
      template-population source is deduplicated. t/375 reproduces
      the race with two forked concurrent calls against the exact
      coderef the dashboard's /move route calls, and proves a manual
      add is untouched - red against pre-fix code (two entries),
      green after. Docker: Files=370, Tests=7503, PASS, 100%
      statement+subroutine coverage on lib/Tira.pm.

3.85  2026-08-24

    - TKT-495. "If the Gate Passing Log length more then 10 start
      pagination. Like click more to load more into that session. So
      do not load all gate log at once." (Michael, on the HTML
      dashboard card dialog.)

    - Fixed: the Gate Passing Log section now renders its first ten
      entries and, when more remain, a "Load more (N more)" button
      that reveals the rest and then hides once every entry is
      shown - rather than rendering the whole log at once regardless
      of length. Evidence and every other list section are
      unaffected; only the Gate Passing Log call site was changed.
      t/playwright/gate-log-pagination.js proves both the
      pagination (ten shown, "Load more" reveals four more, then
      hides) and that a card with nothing logged still renders no
      section - red against pre-fix code (14 entries rendered at
      once), green after. Docker: Files=369, Tests=7499, PASS; the
      full 13-file tools/browser-tests suite passes, including the
      new file.

3.84  2026-08-24

    - TKT-496. Prerequisite for TKT-494 ("It can be multiple
      entries", Michael's own words): column_roles_set stored one
      column per role, so 'entry' meant exactly one column and
      record.create's dispatch (TKT-428) read it as a scalar.

    - Fixed: --role entry=X given more than once now accumulates
      into a list rather than the last one silently winning.
      record.create with no --column lands in the first declared
      entry column; naming any of the declared entries explicitly
      succeeds; a column that is none of them is refused, naming all
      of them. Scoped to the entry role alone - no other role needs
      more than one column for any use case this project has, and
      every board with zero or one entry column is completely
      unaffected. t/374 proves all three scenarios plus full
      backward compatibility. Docker: Files=369, Tests=7499, PASS,
      100% statement+subroutine coverage on lib/Tira.pm and
      lib/Tira/CLI.pm.

3.83  2026-08-24

    - TKT-492. Follow-up to TKT-486: two live "d2 tira.police"
      daemons were found running against the same board at once,
      racing the enforcement ledger. TKT-486/TKT-487 locked the
      write itself, but nothing stopped a second daemon from
      starting in the first place. Michael's answer to that
      question: "d2 tira.police is a singleton process. Whoever the
      last run it is the winner and the loser process will be
      killed."

    - Fixed: the persistent police daemon (_police_follow, not
      --once or policy.bridge) now claims a pid file in the
      violation store before its first round, killing whatever
      still-alive daemon held the claim before it, and releases the
      claim on a clean signal-driven exit. pid/alive/kill are all
      injectable, matching the existing leave/restarter/sleeper
      pattern. t/373 proves the claim-and-kill sequence with injected
      fakes and, in a SKIP-guarded fork, against a real process.
      Docker: Files=368, Tests=7487, PASS, 100%
      statement+subroutine coverage on lib/Tira.pm and
      lib/Tira/CLI.pm.

3.82  2026-08-24

    - TKT-490. Found following TKT-488's own key_details, which
      flagged these two sites as out of scope and noted for a future
      ticket: _indexed_log_read (behind gate.list --id and
      evidence.list --id) and _annotate_log (behind gate.annotate
      and evidence.annotate) shared the identical bug TKT-280 and
      TKT-488 already fixed elsewhere - an unknown id refused with
      only "not found", naming neither the GATE-NNN/EVD-NNN shape
      nor the ids the card actually has.

    - Fixed: both sites now list the card's real ids when it has
      any, or name the shape when it has none, via a new shared
      _unknown_log_entry_message helper - one fix covering gate and
      evidence together, since both are parameterized by the same
      %LOG_SPEC. t/372 proves all four commands. Docker: Files=368,
      Tests=7478, PASS, 100% statement+subroutine coverage on
      lib/Tira.pm and lib/Tira/CLI.pm.

3.81  2026-08-24

    - TKT-353. Reported live: comment.add is the only one of four
      write/read field pairs (gate --details/details, evidence
      --summary/summary, checklist --item/item) where the names
      disagree - comment.add takes --text and the stored/read field
      is called body. A read-back check doing c.get('text') returned
      None silently, indistinguishable from a comment that had not
      landed.

    - Fixed: comment_add, comment_update and comment_list now all
      expose 'text' alongside 'body', the same value - a duplicate
      built fresh at each read boundary rather than a rename (nothing
      can migrate the stored field) and rather than mutating the
      comment object that gets persisted, the same persistence-leak
      shape TKT-407 already found once elsewhere. t/371 proves it,
      including that nothing leaks onto disk. Docker: Files=366,
      Tests=7462, PASS, 100% statement+subroutine coverage on
      lib/Tira.pm and lib/Tira/CLI.pm.

3.80  2026-08-24

    - TKT-298. Reported live: an unknown command gets "Did you mean"
      from the dispatcher; an unknown option got only "Unknown
      option: X" from Getopt::Long, discarded into a generic
      "Invalid command-line options" with nothing suggested. One bad
      option name discarded a whole update carrying twenty composed
      fields, and finding which flag was wrong cost writing a probe
      value into a live card. Also reported: --key-detail (append)
      accepts only the singular while --set-key-details (replace)
      accepts both spellings, because Getopt::Long's abbreviation
      only matches a typed name that is a PREFIX of the declared one.

    - Fixed: an unknown option now suggests the nearest declared
      option name(s) by edit distance - implemented fresh in this
      project's own CLI.pm, since the command-level "Did you mean"
      the ticket pointed at turned out to live in Developer
      Dashboard's own dispatcher, off-limits to modify. Nothing is
      guessed when no declared name is close, and nothing is written
      before the refusal - unchanged. t/370 proves it. Docker:
      Files=365, Tests=7452, PASS, 100% statement+subroutine coverage
      on lib/Tira.pm and lib/Tira/CLI.pm.

3.79  2026-08-24

    - TKT-291. tira.police.outstanding's default output was a flat
      array of strings, and Data::TOON renders that as one inline
      "primitive array" row - every finding comma-joined behind a
      bracketed count and quote marks, so the command an agent is
      told to re-run after every fix ("run tira.police.outstanding
      again and confirm that violation is gone") made a reader parse
      past quoting to find the first thing.

    - Fixed: the default output is now an array of single-key
      {line=>TEXT} hashes, which Data::TOON renders one row per
      finding instead. Added the --by-rule view named in the ticket:
      findings grouped by rule instead of chased/recorded, each card
      listed once per rule even when two policies for the same rule
      matched it, rules somebody should act on sorting before ones
      the board only logs. -o json is unchanged - still the bare list
      the clear-violations instruction pipes. t/369 proves both.
      Docker: Files=364, Tests=7440, PASS, 100%
      statement+subroutine coverage on lib/Tira.pm and
      lib/Tira/CLI.pm.

3.78  2026-08-24

    - TKT-285. Reported live: tira.card.required returned a flat list
      of 14 field names, and the two exceptions to it - a SOW needs
      no parent, a card labelled standalone needs none either -
      existed as prose in tira.usage alone, not in this command's own
      output, not in tira.skills, and tools/card-holes (the push
      gate) carried an independent hardcoded copy of the identical
      exception, a fourth place that could silently drift. A caller
      building a completeness check straight from this command's
      field list, exactly as the manual invites, flagged every
      legitimately parentless card - 169 of 304 live cards on this
      project's own board.

    - Fixed: tira.card.required now answers {"fields": [...],
      "exempt": {"parent": {"types": ["sow"], "labels":
      ["standalone"]}}} instead of a flat list. The engine's own
      card_missing and the push gate's holes() both now read the same
      %CARD_EXEMPT structure instead of each carrying their own copy
      of the exception. Named standalone in SKILLS.md too, which
      previously matched it zero times. t/224, t/227 and the new
      t/368 all prove the shape and the agreement. Docker: Files=363,
      Tests=7430, PASS, 100% statement+subroutine coverage on
      lib/Tira.pm and lib/Tira/CLI.pm.

3.77  2026-08-24

    - TKT-488. Found immediately after shipping TKT-280: the same bug
      in a different array. required_item_update refused an unknown
      id with only "Required item '<id>' not found", naming neither
      the REQ-NNN shape nor the ids the card actually has -
      required-action.update against REQ-NNN ids that shift as cards
      accumulate more required actions is one of the most-run
      commands in this project's own workflow.

    - Fixed: the refusal now lists the card's real ids when it has
      any, or names the REQ-NNN shape when it has none, matching
      checklist_update's TKT-280 fix exactly. t/367 proves both
      branches. Docker: Files=362, Tests=7417, PASS, 100%
      statement+subroutine coverage on lib/Tira.pm and
      lib/Tira/CLI.pm.

3.76  2026-08-24

    - TKT-280. Reported live: "tira.checklist.update --ref REF --id 1
      --status done" against a card whose checklist entries are
      CHK-001..CHK-NNN answered only "Checklist entry '1' not found" -
      true, and it named neither the id shape nor how to discover it.
      An ordinal is the obvious wrong guess, because entries print as
      a numbered list; a caller looping ordinals believing --id took
      the position failed silently on every call and reported three
      cards' checklists as ticked when none of them were.

    - Fixed: the refusal now lists the card's real ids when it has
      any, or names the CHK-NNN shape when it has none - the tool
      already knows the valid ids at the moment it refuses. t/366
      proves both branches. Docker: Files=361, Tests=7409, PASS,
      100% statement+subroutine coverage on lib/Tira.pm and
      lib/Tira/CLI.pm.

3.75  2026-08-24

    - TKT-487. Found while implementing TKT-486: grepping for other
      writers to the enforcement ledger file turned up two more
      unlocked read-modify-writes of the exact same file -
      _announce_moves (the move-notification stamp) and
      _agent_still_mark_notified (the agent-still throttle stamp).
      Neither was covered by TKT-486's lock, since it only wrapped
      violation_record's own call. A stale read in either could
      silently overwrite what any of the three writers had just
      recorded.

    - Fixed: factored TKT-486's inline lock out into a shared
      _with_enforcement_lock helper, now used by violation_record,
      _announce_moves and _agent_still_mark_notified alike, so all
      three serialise against each other. t/365 proves both newly
      wrapped functions hold the lock across their own read and
      write, that a failure releases it, and that one writer's own
      pass no longer clobbers another writer's already-recorded
      state on the same file. Docker: Files=360, Tests=7400, PASS,
      100% statement+subroutine coverage on lib/Tira.pm and
      lib/Tira/CLI.pm.

3.74  2026-08-24

    - TKT-486. Investigated live: ps aux showed two "d2 tira.police"
      daemons and five stray "d2 tira.policy.bridge" tails running
      against the same board at once. violation_record - the function
      every police pass uses to update the enforcement ledger's
      escalation state - read the whole ledger, mutated an in-memory
      copy, and wrote the whole thing back with no lock, contradicting
      its own code comment ("Police writes here and nobody else
      does"). A second daemon whose read landed before the first
      daemon's write could silently erase the first daemon's
      already-recorded violation, indistinguishable from that
      violation never having been announced.

    - Fixed: violation_record now holds an exclusive lock, scoped to
      the violation store, across its own read and write - the same
      shape _with_project_lock already gives every other
      read-modify-write in this project, applied here to the one
      place that was still missing it. t/364 proves the lock is held
      during the read (not just the write) and that a failure
      partway through still releases it. Whether preventing a second
      police daemon from starting against the same board at all is
      also needed is asked as Q-067 on TKT-486; this release covers
      the write-corruption race the lock closes. Docker: Files=359,
      Tests=7388, PASS, 100% statement+subroutine coverage on
      lib/Tira.pm and lib/Tira/CLI.pm.

3.73  2026-08-24

    - TKT-485. Reported live: tira.ticket.create landing in a column
      with required_actions wrote the record first, then populated
      required_items via required_item_add - which requires
      --author. A create with no author (and no TIRA_AUTHOR) failed
      after the record already existed: an orphaned, unattributed
      card on disk, and a retry with --author created a second,
      duplicate ticket for the same work rather than completing the
      first.

    - Fixed: record.create now refuses before writing, not after,
      when the landing column carries required_actions and no author
      is available from --author or TIRA_AUTHOR - naming the same
      message required_item_add already raised, just moved before
      any write happens. A column with no required_actions is
      unaffected either way. Docker: Files=358, Tests=7379, PASS,
      100% statement+subroutine coverage on lib/Tira.pm and
      lib/Tira/CLI.pm. Browser tests 12/12.

3.72  2026-08-24

    - TKT-437. card-unlinked and parent-ahead-of-children both depend
      on column_roles(type)->{done} to know which cards have already
      shipped, and policy_unresolved's own unresolved-policy
      detector only ever warned about one of them - hardcoded to
      parent-ahead-of-children by rule name, so card-unlinked
      declared with no done role failed the identical silent way
      with no warning at all. Second bug found investigating the
      first: the fix command in the unresolved detail hardcoded
      "--type ticket" regardless of the policy's own --type, so a
      parent-ahead-of-children policy declared --type epic was told
      the exact right diagnosis and the wrong fix.

    - Fixed: the detector now covers card-unlinked too, and names
      the policy's own --type in the fix command (a neutral
      "--type TYPE" placeholder when the policy applies to all
      three). Docker: Files=358, Tests=7379, PASS, 100%
      statement+subroutine coverage on lib/Tira.pm and
      lib/Tira/CLI.pm. Browser tests 12/12.

3.71  2026-08-23

