3.31  2026-08-22

    - TKT-475. Removing a column - via tira.column.remove, or via the
      Columns dialog's Save button (column_apply) - never scrubbed that
      column's name out of other columns' stored --next chain arrays.
      A dangling reference then made the chain check treat the column
      that pointed there as still forking to a column that no longer
      existed: any forward move not in the now-partly-nonexistent fork
      list refused, and the refusal's own suggested remedy ("move there
      first") failed too, since the named column had nowhere to move
      to. Every card in the affected column had no valid forward move
      at all. Found by the hourly-hunt agent's first real run (TKT-474).

    - Fixed: column_remove and column_apply now strip a removed
      column's name from every other column's next array. column_apply
      also strips any next entry naming a column absent from the
      layout being saved, even if the caller's own payload still names
      it - the dialog's Next checkboxes are a snapshot taken when it
      opened and are not refreshed by another row's removal in the same
      session, so a stale selection can otherwise reach the server.
      column_update now refuses --next naming a column that does not
      exist, rather than accepting a typo silently.

