3.17  2026-08-21

    - TKT-457. Chain-order and required-action enforcement (TKT-426,
      TKT-452) live only in Tira::CLI's record.move dispatch - deliberate,
      so a signed-in browser move stays exempt. But that also meant any
      caller reaching record_move some other way than the CLI - a script,
      an integration, a raw API call - skipped both checks entirely, with
      no record of who or what moved the card. Confirmed in production: two
      moves with no author recorded skipped nine columns, neither check
      ever running.

    - Fixed: record_move itself now refuses a caller that supplies no
      author, or one naming somebody the project has never registered -
      the same standard --assignee and --reporter already meet. The CLI
      already resolved --author or TIRA_AUTHOR before this; reaching
      record_move with neither is now refused with a corrective message
      rather than silently recorded against nobody. A signed-in browser
      move is unaffected. record_discard and record_restore inherit the
      refusal, since both call record_move underneath. Scoped to
      record_move alone, by the owner's own answer to this ticket's scope
      question - other mutating commands are separate, later tickets.

3.16  2026-08-21

    - TKT-456. column_add positions a new column with --after/--before, but
      that only affects display/derived order - it never touched a
      neighboring column's own explicit --next. When that neighbor's next
      was already set before the insertion (rather than derived from
      position), the new column became structurally present but
      unreachable via the declared chain, and a card could skip straight
      past it with no refusal and no warning.

    - Fixed: column_add now leaves an advisory warning (via warning_add,
      the same channel a failed reminder delivery uses) when the column
      immediately before the insertion point has an explicit next that
      does not already include the new column - naming the unreachable
      column, its neighbor, and the column.update --next to fix it. The
      column is still created; this does not refuse the insertion.

3.15  2026-08-21

    - TKT-459. project_update's --agent was validated by a closure in
      %AUTOMATION_SETTING that checked the value against the literal
      string 'claude' - a leftover from before any other agent existed.
      Any board wanting to declare card-changed-by-owner for a
      differently-named agent (zenbot, codex, anything but literally
      'claude') was refused outright, with no way to name who actually
      works the board.

    - Fixed: --agent is now validated the same way --assignee and
      --reporter already are, via _require_active_person - it must name a
      real, active person on the project. project_new's onboarding
      answers "who works this board" (--members) and "which of them is
      the agent" (--agent) as two separate questions, so an agent named
      there but not separately listed among --members is now
      auto-registered as a person, matching what the wizard has always
      let through in practice.

3.14  2026-08-21

    - TKT-454. column_apply - the engine call behind the browser
      dashboard's Columns dialog Save button - already read a column's
      chain (next) and required-action template (required_actions) back
      via _column_defaults, but never wrote either. A layout
      round-tripped through the dialog silently dropped both: open it,
      change nothing about the chain or template, hit Save, and they
      vanish.

    - Fixed: column_apply now persists next and required_actions per
      column, matching what it already read back. Adding editable fields
      for both to the Columns dialog itself is tracked separately; this
      release closes the underlying data-loss gap, which existed even for
      a caller that passed both fields through directly.

3.13  2026-08-21

    - TKT-455. Backward-move required-action reset stopped one column
      short of where the card actually landed. The owner's own example:
      moving a card from column 4 to column 2 should reset required items
      for columns 4, 3 AND 2 - the destination the card lands on, not
      just what it passed through on the way. Until this release the
      destination was deliberately excluded (t/309 tested exactly this
      exclusion), so an item already done there stayed done even though
      the card was landing back on that exact column.

    - Fixed: the backward-reset range now runs from the destination
      column through the origin, inclusive on both ends. Every other
      column's reset behavior, and the forward-move population branch,
      are unaffected.

3.12  2026-08-21

    - TKT-453. Marking a checklist or required-action item done proved
      nothing. Caught on ZSD-246: an agent created 9 checklist items and
      marked all 9 Done inside a 5-second window, a narrative written after
      the work supposedly finished rather than a live record of progress -
      the third time on the same ticket a "done" claim was not
      substantively true when made.

    - Fixed: checklist.update and required-action.update now refuse
      --status done (case-insensitively) without at least one
      --command/--proof pair, repeatable for an item that took several
      commands to satisfy. --proof is the literal output of the paired
      --command, trusted as given. Every pair is stored on the item and
      logged to gate_passing_log. Proof over 2000 characters (the same
      truncation threshold already used elsewhere on this board) is stored
      as an attachment instead of inlined, referenced by filename. Every
      other status change, including the move mechanism's own backward
      reset to pending, is unaffected.

    - Also fixed, found along the way: t/70-doc-examples.t's own
      command-name extraction excluded hyphens, silently truncating
      hyphenated commands like required-action.update to "required" -
      invisible until this release's own --command flag happened to
      collide with a word in the resulting garbled error message.

3.11  2026-08-21

    - TKT-398. notify_moves wrote to the board when asked only to read it.
      MEASURED while diagnosing why move notifications never delivered:
      tira.project.show showed no notify_moves setting, then a bare
      tira.notify.moves (a read) made the key appear - invented by nobody's
      decision. The engine method used `||=` to default the setting and
      called _write_yaml unconditionally; the CLI dispatch compounded it by
      always passing enabled (defaulting to 1 when --watch was absent), so
      even a genuinely bare `d2 tira.notify.moves` looked like a request to
      enable it by the time it reached the engine. The harm: asking "has
      anybody turned this on?" destroyed the answer by being asked.

    - Fixed: both layers now only persist a change when one was actually
      named - a chat, a column, or --watch/--no-watch. A bare read reports
      the current setting, or the untouched default (enabled: false),
      without writing anything to the board.

3.10  2026-08-21

    - TKT-452. Browser dashboard card moves never populated or reset required
      actions - only moves through the CLI/agent path did. browser_providers's
      move handler called $tira->record_move() directly, skipping
      _apply_column_required_actions entirely; that function was only ever
      reachable from the CLI/agent dispatch path (record.move inside
      _invoke). A card dragged into a column with required actions never
      received its template, and dragging one backward left a required item
      marked done exactly as it was. Raised from a live question: "when card
      move backward, why the required actions on the card not being
      resetted?"

    - Fixed: the browser move provider now calls the same population/reset
      logic the CLI/agent path already calls, right after record_move
      succeeds. TKT-426's CLI/agent-only *gating* (refusing a move whose
      required actions are undone, and the column-chain-order refusal) is
      unchanged - a human on the dashboard still is not an agent skipping a
      gate. Only the bookkeeping half - keeping the required-action list
      accurate for whoever looks at the card next - now happens on both
      paths identically.

3.09  2026-08-21

    - TKT-391. priority-skipped's violation message could disprove its own
      arithmetic. _outranks_for_work settles a tie in priority by age -
      correctly, the one that has waited longer should go first - but the
      message only had one sentence available: "waits at priority N, above
      this card's N". When the age tie-break was what actually fired, both
      cards sat at the SAME priority, and the message printed two equal
      numbers while claiming one outranked the other. Measured on this
      board: "waits at priority 5, above this card's 5" - a correct finding
      with a false reason, which sent the reader chasing an off-by-one that
      did not exist.

    - Fixed: _outranks_for_work now returns which fact actually decided the
      comparison, not just whether it outranks. When priority decided, the
      message is unchanged. When the age tie-break decided, the message now
      says the card "waits at the same priority and has been waiting since"
      the date that settles it, instead of claiming a priority difference
      that was never there.

3.08  2026-08-20

    - TKT-412. question.mark/answer/update/discard refused a missing --id
      with "A question reference is required - supply it with --ref" - the
      CLI's own translation table pointed the reader at the wrong flag,
      since the field the die actually checks is always the question's own
      id, never --ref. A caller who mistook --ref for the question's own
      reference (and never supplied --id) got the identical message as one
      who supplied nothing at all, and could not tell "you gave nothing"
      from "you gave the wrong thing" from the message alone.

    - Fixed: the engine message now reads "A question id is required" and
      the CLI hints --id. When --ref itself holds something shaped like a
      question id (Q-NNN) while --id is missing, the refusal now says so
      distinctly, naming what was given. A --ref naming a genuinely
      different real card is unaffected - the existing cross-check message
      already told that case apart.

3.07  2026-08-20

    - TKT-434. docs/commands.md documents checklist and required-action
      status as free text, and the engine validates only that it is
      non-empty. But four places compared a status against the exact
      lowercase string 'done' rather than case-insensitively: the
      card-stalled and checklist-unmoved police rules, and the
      required-action move-out gate and its backward-reset. A checklist or
      required item marked --status Done or --status DONE read as complete
      to any person looking at the board, but was silently invisible to
      all four: card-stalled never reported the stall, checklist-unmoved
      kept reporting the item as outstanding, and a move refused with a
      confusing "not done" message even though it plainly said Done.
      Duplicate report TKT-444 (zen-framework) gave a concrete live repro:
      ZSD-233's item marked --status Done refused a move that
      --status done (lowercase only) then let straight through with
      nothing else changed.

    - Fixed: all four sites now compare with lc(...) eq 'done', so Done,
      DONE, dOne, etc. are recognized everywhere 'done' gates a rule.
      Every other value, such as todo, is unaffected - the fix is a
      case-insensitive comparison, not a new restriction.

3.06  2026-08-20

    - TKT-433. Creation seeded a birth entry for every FIELD a create call
      populated, but not for the STARTING COLUMN - column is not a stored
      record field, it is the directory the file sits in, so the generic
      per-field journal never saw it. A card created directly into a
      non-default column had no history entry recording it ever arrived
      there, and column-skipped - which reads exactly that history to see
      which columns a card visited - flagged the card as having skipped
      every column up to the one it was actually created in. Reported
      from mt5-ai, hit five different cards across one session on our own
      board before this shipped.

    - Fixed: create_record now writes a manual history entry for the
      starting column (op => 'create', before => null, after => the
      column), mirroring record_move's own existing manual entry for a
      real move, tagged distinctly so the two remain distinguishable.
      Fixing this surfaced a second bug: three existing consumers of
      column history (agent-still's stopped-agent detection, move-
      announcement notifications, and checklist-unmoved's advanced-
      without-ticking check) matched on the journal's field name alone
      and would have misread every new creation as a real move - all
      three now also require the move op specifically.

3.05  2026-08-20

    - TKT-440. Required-action items (TKT-445) had a data model but no
      visibility: the card dialog rendered only the general checklist,
      with no way to see which items a column actually requires or how
      many are done, without reading the JSON directly.

    - The card dialog now shows a "Required actions (done/total)"
      section, grouped by column, the moment a card with any is opened
      - separate from the general checklist, which is unaffected. A
