      not twice by two rules disagreeing about which number was true.

2.73  2026-08-18

    - TKT-352. policy.add has refused a second policy on the same rule and
      scope since 2.54 (TKT-339). That does nothing for a board that already
      carried the duplicate before the refusal existed - a board upgrading
      into the fix keeps every pair it already had, and nothing said so.

    - Measured on one board: four pairs, all declared 2026-08-14 minutes
      apart - a bulk re-declaration that could not have known the earlier
      ones were there. In every pair the earlier policy carried a written
      message and the later one did not, so the duplicate was silently
      overriding a considered declaration with a bare one; on one pair it
      also tightened 15m to 10m with nobody deciding it - TKT-339's own
      complaint, reintroduced by a duplicate the fix could not see
      retroactively. Found by grouping `tira.policy.list -o json` by hand
      after reading the changelog for what "same scope" meant.

    - policy_duplicates runs the identical scope comparison policy_add
      already makes against a new declaration, once across everything
      already stored, and groups what collides. policy.review now names
      the groups under a `duplicates` key - the one place a reader already
      checks for what the policy catalogue adds up to, rather than a
      separate command to remember.

    - Detection only: which member of a colliding pair to keep is a
      judgment call, and guessing it here risks discarding a policy
      somebody actually wanted. The scope-field list itself moved to one
      shared @POLICY_SCOPE_FIELDS constant, so _policy_already_declared and
      policy_duplicates cannot drift into two different definitions of a
      collision.

2.72  2026-08-18

    - TKT-351. A release ran the full suite twice on the same tree, once by
      hand and once again in the push hook, because the hook had no way to
      know the tree it was about to check out was the one just proved.
      Measured on one release tonight: the gate passed - 258 files, 6072
      tests, 100.0 on all three modules - and the push hook then ran the
      identical suite against the identical tree, taking 28 minutes on top of
      it. Only one test container may run at a time, so six cards and five
      releases queued behind that from 19:44 past midnight, and three
      attempts were killed at a ceiling that had to be raised before any
      could finish - every one of them a second run of something already
      proved.

    - The hook was not wrong to distrust a claim - "I ran it and it passed"
      is a sentence, and a gate that accepts sentences is not a gate. What it
      can trust instead is a record naming the exact tree it tested, in
      git's own tree hash: the hash git computes from the actual blob
      contents, which nobody can produce for a tree they never had.

    - tools/gate-run tests a checkout of HEAD - the same commit the hook
      itself checks out, never the working desk - and on a real pass at 100%
      coverage on all three modules writes a record under that tree's hash,
      shared across worktrees via `git rev-parse --git-common-dir`.
      tools/gate-cache-read is the only thing that consults it: a hit
      requires the record's own claimed tree to match the filename it is
      stored under (not merely the filename matching), and to be recent
      enough (GATE_CACHE_MAX_AGE, default 3600s) to trust without asking
      again. Anything else - absent, a different tree, gone stale - and the
      push hook runs the suite exactly as it always has.

    - The hook writes a record after a live run too, not only tools/gate-run,
      so a push that fails at a later step (documentation, browser) can
      retry without paying for the suite again.

    - What the gate refuses is unchanged, and how long the suite itself
      takes is TKT-350's - this only stops asking the same question of the
      same tree twice.

