2.48  2026-08-17

    - TKT-308, asked for directly: "on the bridge, each line of message show
      for <who> - it never guessed right, and when wrong the agent ignores it
      instead of inspecting it first. can you remove that part of the line."

    - The failure mode is the one that matters. A wrong addressee does not
      merely fail to help: it gives every other reader a reason to skip the
      line, so a guess that was right most of the time would still cost more
      than it saves - the cost lands entirely on the times it is wrong.

    - And it was a guess: inferred from the card, the assignee if there was one
      and "anyone" if there was not, which is why lines about cards nobody was
      assigned to read "for anyone" and were skipped by everybody.

    - Gone from every line, including the STILL OPEN tail, where it was most
      misleading - that line's whole job is to stop things being forgotten and
      it was addressed to a name a reader could decide was not theirs.

    - Which reader a line reaches is deliberately unchanged, and keeping it
      that way was most of the work: the routing was reading the addressee
      back out of the line, so removing the words would have quietly stopped
      every agent-scoped read from filtering at all. Three tests caught it.
      Whose a line is now comes from the store, through the reference the line
      already carries - and the summary tail, which carries no reference on
      purpose, is routed on the cards it names.

