2.40  2026-08-17

    - TKT-279, reported by developer-dashboard: tira.police.outstanding
      exited 0 whether the board was clean or carrying violations, so a job
      scheduled to run it could not alarm on the result. That is how 154
      violations accumulated there over two days unnoticed - 153 of them
      log-only, so none had reached the bridge the agent watches.
    - --exit-nonzero-if-any makes the status carry the answer: 1 while
      anything is outstanding, 0 when clear, and an error still exits 2, so
      the three answers a checker needs - clean, findings, could-not-look -
      are all expressible. Opt-in rather than a changed default, because a
      command that starts exiting non-zero breaks every script running it
      today; the precedent is --if-changed, which already does this here.
    - The grouped view that report also asked for is not in this release. It
      was written and works, and it cost one statement of coverage I could not
      locate in six attempts - so it is split onto its own card rather than
      holding up the exit status, which is the half a scheduled job needs
      tonight.
    - One correction to the report, recorded on the card. It said the command
      prints one record per sighting and the same card appears a dozen times.
      It does not - one row per violation with a seen count, and three passes
      over the same fault still give one row. The volume is real; the cause is
      the rendering, so the answer is a summary mode rather than deduplication.

2.39  2026-08-17

    - TKT-273: each change of version is announced once, rather than every
      difference announced for ever. Comparing for difference was right for
      one watcher and wrong for two - and two is the documented arrangement,
      police in the owner's terminal and a bridge the agent tails, both
      running a pass. At different versions each saw a value that was not its
      own, announced an upgrade and wrote its own: "Tira is now 2.35 - this
      board last heard 2.34" at 23:16:42 and the exact reverse nine seconds
      later, still going eight minutes on.
    - Announcing only a newer version was the obvious fix and was wrong. The
      existing test asserts a rollback is announced too, and its comment says
      that assertion was written after getting it the other way round: the
      rules the agent learned about may not be there any more. That fix was
      reverted the moment the suite said so.
    - What the board records is what has been SAID, not merely what was last
      seen. A change from one version to another is news the first time and
      not the second, so two watchers taking turns say it twice and stop for
      ever, while a move to something this board has not been told about is
      still announced.

2.38  2026-08-17

    - TKT-278, his question: "is there any policy to remind the agent a card
      has been sitting on the same column without any changes for too long?"
      Partly, and the missing half was the important one. card-duration
      measures how long a card has been in one NAMED column and says nothing
      about whether anybody is working it; checklist-idle watches the
      checklist alone; board-still asks the right question - has anything
      happened - of a whole board, and there was no per-card version. So a
      card moved into verify twenty minutes ago and abandoned was
      indistinguishable from one moved in twenty minutes ago and being
      verified right now.
    - card-still reports a card nothing has happened to for longer than an
      age, in any column work happens in, reading the same last_updated stamp
      board-still reads for a board - every kind of activity touches it. No
      column to name, so one policy covers what previously took five.
    - Columns work does not happen in are left alone. A card resting in the
      backlog has not started and a card in an ending is over, and reporting
      those would put every board permanently in violation of its own
      history.
    - Proved by measuring dwell instead: with the rule reading when the card
      arrived in its column rather than when anything last happened to it, a
      card being worked that minute is reported - which is the noise this
      exists to avoid, and the difference the question turned on.
    - And each column says how long is too long, which was his refinement:
      "there is a protection-card option to set for it... how long it can
      stay in backlog; or some columns called done, or released ones - those
      don't need setting, so there won't be spamming messages." Every column
      has carried its own limit in minutes and its own watched flag since
      they were added; tira.stale has judged cards by them all along and no
      rule ever had. card-still asks the column first, falls back to the age
      the policy was declared with, and leaves an unwatched column alone
      however old its cards are.

2.37  2026-08-16

    - TKT-276, his request: an upgrade is traced until it is finished rather
      than announced and forgotten. The upgrade notice asked the agent to do
      three things and was then gone - no reference, no escalation, nothing
      that noticed whether any of it happened. He tested it by hand across
      four agents: "They all answered partially." Then he watched this agent
      do the same, four notices in one day, none acted on, while a rule sat
      undeclared on the board the whole time.
    - rules-undeclared reports a rule this board has neither declared nor
      declined, with a reference and the same escalation every other rule
      has, and settles only when every rule has an answer. Declining counts:
      the point is that nothing is left unconsidered.
    - It traces the part that can be observed and claims nothing else.
      Whether the changes were read cannot be checked, and a rule that
      settles on a promise is worse than no rule - so what is watched is the
      gap the reading is for, which tira.policy.undeclared already answers.
    - Answering some of them leaves it open, which is the whole of the
      question he asked.
    - TKT-277, also his: a settlement is not an ending, and the line has to
      say so. "Most agents think the last settle statement as end and all
      done and forget everything." A settlement is written in the shape of a
      violation and arrives last, so it reads as a closing statement - and
      this board had 84 violations outstanding while settlements were
      landing on the same channel. Every pass that writes anything now ends
      with how many are still open and which, and a pass with nothing to say
      still writes nothing, because silence has to go on meaning silence.
    - The tail obeys the action, which the suite caught at once: without that
      filter a board whose rules are all log-only - a policy being tuned,
      deliberately out of the way - started getting bridge lines about them,
      breaking the promise that only what a policy asked for reaches the
      agent.
    - Three catalogue guards caught the new rule, which is what they are for:
      the guide must show it, two tests keep their own list of every rule,
      and one asserts every declared rule fires against a board built to
      break them all. That last one cannot hold for this rule - its subject
      is a rule left unanswered, and that board answers every rule - so it is
      exempt there by name and with the reason, and the exemption itself is
      asserted so it cannot grow unnoticed.

2.36  2026-08-16

    - TKT-273: an upgrade is announced once, however many watchers there
      are. police recorded which version it had announced and compared it
      with its own for DIFFERENCE - and a difference has two directions.
      Two watchers at different versions each saw a value that was not
      theirs, announced an upgrade and wrote their own, so the record
      flipped and the notice arrived for ever. Measured on this board with
      five watchers running: "Tira is now 2.35 - this board last heard
      2.34" at 23:16:42, and the exact reverse at 23:16:51.
    - The guard's own comment says it is written once per version rather
      than once per start, "because a line written on every start would
      arrive on a loop" - which is precisely what two watchers reproduced.
      A check that exists and cannot hold.
    - Not fixed with a lock, because two watchers is the documented
      arrangement: police in the owner's terminal and a bridge the agent
      tails, both running a pass. The board records the newest version any
      watcher has announced, so an older watcher is behind rather than
      upgraded and has nothing to say.
    - Compared piece by piece rather than as text, because 2.9 and 2.35 are
      the wrong way round as strings and the right way round as releases,
      and this project has been through both of those numbers.

2.35  2026-08-16

    - TKT-269: a card that says it stands alone is not an orphan. Whether a
