2.23  2026-08-16

    - TKT-261: a string that looks like a number is still a string. TOON is
      the default output and the one every agent reads, and Data::TOON
      tests a scalar against a number pattern before it tests whether it
      needs quoting - so a fix version of 2.20 was handed to the reader as
      2.2, a different release. 1.10 read as 1.1, 1.50 as 1.5, 1.90 as 1.9.
      Every release whose version ends in a zero has always come back as
      another one.
    - Measured rather than argued: 20,732 string values on this project's
      board, 22 of which did not survive a round trip through the default
      output, and 19 of those a fix_version. The value on disk was right
      the whole time, and the JSON output the tests read is correct - which
      is why nothing in the suite ever noticed. It was found by accident,
      stamping a card with the release it went out in.
    - The module has the right rule and cannot reach it: its own
      _needs_quoting returns true for a numeric-looking string, and
      _encode_primitive returns the canonicalised number before ever
      asking. The order is restored here for scalars Perl still knows are
      strings, and only where leaving the value alone would change it, so a
      number stays a number and ordinary output is untouched.

2.22  2026-08-16

    - TKT-253: a finished release is no longer held up by the work that
      follows it. The push gate asked the card check about every card on
      the board, which is a different question from whether the commit
      being pushed is fit to go - and the answer changes while the push
      runs. It refused 2.14 three times for cards that had nothing to do
      with it, and refused 2.21 for a card a bug hunt had raised an hour
      earlier on an unrelated subject. The gate now asks about the cards
      the commits being pushed name, read from their subjects the same way
      the commit gate reads them. Whether the board as a whole is in order
      is police's question, asked continuously on a channel somebody
      reads.
    - Narrowing what a check looks at is not weakening it: a commit whose
      own card is a title is refused exactly as before, and a narrowed run
      says which cards it was asked about, because a check that quietly
      examines less reads as one that examined everything.
    - tools/prove-the-gate found two faults in this change that reading it
      did not. The refs are collected with a grep, and a grep that matches
      nothing exits non-zero - under the hook's own `set -e` that killed
      the whole gate silently at the card check, which eleven probes
      reported as the gate being broken. And every probe here read the
      output of the last hook run rather than of the command it had just
      run, so the first probe that ran something other than the hook was
      reported as refusing without saying why. Both now proved: 23 refusals
      exercised, none unmeasured.

2.21  2026-08-16

    - TKT-249: a board is backed up if anything has backed it up.
      board-unbacked read the board's own git commit, which is what
      tira.backup writes, and only looked at the dated export directories
      when there was no commit at all. The push gate writes those
      directories on every push - 481 of them for this board, the newest
      twenty minutes old - and the rule was answering with a commit six
      hours older, then advising the reader to run the command whose
      answer it was already using. The later of the two is the answer,
      because the question is when this board was last backed up rather
      than which mechanism did it.
    - The two were also on different clocks, which only mattered once they
      were compared. The commit time was read as a local wall clock with
      the offset thrown away and a Z put on the end, so a backup at
      01:03+01:00 was recorded as 01:03Z - an hour later than it happened,
      labelled with the one timezone it was not in. Against an age measured
      in days that was slack; against a gate stamping its directories in
      real UTC it means the older backup can win. It is read as an instant
      now.

2.20  2026-08-16

    - TKT-230: the push gate has seventeen ways to refuse and four of them
      had never been seen to refuse. The one that mattered: the browser
      step was proved for a runner that is ABSENT and never for a browser
      test that RUNS AND FAILS - and a failing browser test is what
      happened, on the release that broke the served dashboard while ten
      browser tests passed. Both are now provoked deliberately, along with
      a board backup that fails rather than one that is missing.
    - The two that remain unprovoked are a missing compose file and a
      checkout that fails. Provoking either means damaging the machine the
      gate runs on, and this file has already twice left the thing it was
      checking in a worse state, so each carries a written reason instead
      of a gap.
    - t/233 makes the count instead of a person making it. It reads the
      hook for the ways it can refuse and the prover for what answers each
      one, so a gate that grows a step is not covered by a list somebody
      forgot to extend. I miscounted this by hand twice while raising the
      card, both times in the tool's favour.
    - And it caught a fault in its own first fix. The guard added so that a
      probe blocked by an incomplete card says so rather than reporting the
      gate broken also silenced the one probe that makes the board
      incomplete on purpose - the hollow card, the only probe that puts a
      real card on the real board. It was skipped from the moment the guard
      was written, and the tool's count did not notice, because a skip was
      neither proved nor failed. A probe that could not run is now counted
      and named in the closing line, and the tool no longer reports success
      for what it did not measure.

2.19  2026-08-16

    - TKT-245: a report another project filed does not stop this one
