2.03  2026-08-15

    - TKT-211: an example the collector never finds is no longer invisible.
      tools/docs-examples-run collected a fenced example only if it had at
      most two leading spaces and the token after the command began with a
      dash. Both documents indent worked examples by four, and both
      annotate them - "d2 tira.doctor    # what is damaged, and where" - so
      nineteen distinct examples were in neither number the summary
      reports: not run, not set aside, and invisible to the push gate that
      reads that summary as the documentation having been checked. 278
      examples now run where 254 did.
    - The trailing comment is the sharper half. The runner has always
      handled one - shlex.split(line, comments=True), whose own comment
      says a trailing "# set it" is a note to the reader rather than an
      argument - so the code that RUNS an example believed examples carry
      comments while the code that FINDS them could not match a single
      one. Two copies of one decision, drifted apart, and the blind half
      was the half doing the counting.
    - The pattern is deliberately not widened to any trailing text. A line
      of prose beginning with a command name would then be collected and
      run; there is an assertion holding that line.
    - Widening a pattern fixes one variation and leaves the next to be
      found the same way - which is how this was found, the previous fix
      having widened it for the d2 form. So the check now accounts for
      every example line: an independent reading of the documents, minus
      everything either run or set aside, must be empty, and whatever is
      left is named rather than counted.
    - t/155 selected the summary by being the first statement with that
      prefix, which held for as long as there was one. There are two now,
      and it had silently moved onto the other. It selects by what the
      line says.

2.02  2026-08-15

    - TKT-210: a command run quietly is no longer heard by everyone the
      caller was talking to. _running_quietly leaked the child's output
      onto the process's real standard output whenever the caller had
      captured its own - which is every test in this suite and the served
      dashboard collecting a response - so "git version 2.52.0" could
      arrive in the middle of a command's output.
    - The cause is on the parent's side of the fork, not the child's.
      open3 silences a child by reopening the STDOUT and STDERR globs
      after forking, and that moves descriptors 1 and 2 only while those
      globs still own them. A captured caller leaves the glob with no
      descriptor at all, so nothing reaches descriptor 1 and exec passes
      the real one through. No choice of open3 argument fixes it: a
      handle, a fileno dup string and a second null device were each
      tried and each leaked identically. The descriptors themselves are
      now pointed at the null device around the call and put back after,
      so the child inherits harmless ones whatever the globs are doing.
    - The same hole was on the error stream. t/139 never reached it
      because it reopens STDERR onto a real file, which hands descriptor
      2 back a real descriptor and hides the fault - the same way an
      early attempt at the new test went green by aiming descriptor 1 at
      a file. Both streams are covered now.
    - The card's own description was wrong and is corrected on the board.
      It claimed the helper closed the caller's output and that
      everything printed afterwards was lost; both prints either side of
      the call arrive intact. Three tests passed while the fault was
      present before one measured it in the only way that can: with a
      real descriptor 1 pointed somewhere readable and an in-memory glob
      at the same time.

2.01  2026-08-15

    - TKT-209: a violation says which board it belongs to. Two people
      looked up VIO-0453 on the same morning and got different answers -
      card-sandbox-missing on DD-532 from one board, column-skipped on
      M5T-365 from another - and neither had any way to notice.
    - Every board's store counts its own violations from one, which is
      right: within a board the number does its job, making one lasting
      problem read as one problem getting louder rather than as noise
      repeating. What went wrong is that the number escaped the board,
      into reports filed between projects and into what an agent pastes
      to its owner, and three projects file into this board now.
    - His decision, given three options: a field at the end of the line
      plus the board named in the replay header. At the end because
      mt5-ai and developer-dashboard have both written tooling against
      that line, and a reader splitting the first fields sees exactly
      what it saw before. Making the numbers unique across boards was
      rejected - a board's store is its own, and coordinating them is
      what this design avoids.
    - The board is remembered by the store when it is written to, rather
      than passed in at read time, so a tail started an hour later still
      names it.
    - And a cost that only appeared once it was built, now documented: a
      line carries a name somebody chose, so a board called Settled,
      Done or Urgent matches a grep for that word anywhere in the line.
      A test whose board is named Settled had three assertions match
      every line the moment the name arrived. Matchers should key on the
      field - the line has always been shaped for it - and the reference
      says so rather than leaving the next reader to find out.

2.00  2026-08-15

    - TKT-129 (dashboard half): a served board picks up new code. The
      other half of the report that began "when installed new Tira in
      new version, dashboard and police won't reload themselves" - the
      police half shipped in 1.98.
    - It was written down here as impossible, and that was reasoned from
      the failure we hit rather than from the tool. He sent me to
      Starman's documentation: HUP restarts workers gracefully while the
      master keeps the listening socket, so nothing has to exec and
      nothing has to rebind.
    - What stopped HUP working was ours. serve() built the application
      in the launching process and handed Plack::Runner a coderef, so
      the app was in memory before Starman forked and re-forked workers
      inherited it. The board is now served from dashboard.psgi, which
      each worker loads for itself, so a HUP re-forks workers that read
      the modules from disk.
    - Proved before choosing: a two-worker Starman serving a .psgi that
      read a version from a file answered "version one"; the file was
      changed, the master was sent HUP, and it answered "version two" -
      same master, socket never closed. His decision, given the choice,
      was to prove that rather than add Server::Starter, which is the
      documented answer for an application that genuinely must be
      preloaded and would have put a dependency in every project that
      installs Tira.
    - dashboard.psgi has shipped since it was written with nothing
      referencing it - no test, no command - so a change to the
      providers could have broken it silently. It is now loaded by the
      suite, which is how it was found that serve() had never been given
      the board at all: the workers load the file themselves and cannot
      be handed a closure over it, so the project, the type and the
      title travel in the environment, and serving without a project is
      refused rather than starting workers that die on load.

1.99  2026-08-15

    - TKT-187: a card that shipped no release can say so. His decision,
      answering a question on the card: 'none' is the reserved value on
      fix_version, meaning nothing was released for this card.
    - Two cards on this project's own board had made the case. TKT-006
      and TKT-071 were in done with every checklist item ticked and no
      version, and card-metrics reported them eighteen times each,
      reaching CRITICAL. Neither appears in any commit or in this file,
      because neither shipped a release - TKT-071's whole deliverable
      was asking seven questions and getting them answered, and all
      seven were. Writing a version on either would have put a number on
      the card that is not true, and a violation nobody can close is the
      complaint mt5-ai raised the same morning about card-damaged.
    - The engine needed no change, which reading the rule established
      before anything was built: card-metrics tests emptiness, so 'none'
      already satisfied it. What was missing is what makes it an answer
      rather than a silence, and that is documented here: 'n/a', '-' and
      'not released' would satisfy the rule too, and if every card
      invents its own word then nobody can ask how many cards shipped
      nothing, and 'none' quietly becomes a way of switching the rule
      off one card at a time.
    - So the word is written down, the cards claiming it are countable
      with one command, and the line it must not blur is stated: 'none'
      says the work finished and shipped nothing, not that the version
      is not known yet - a card that will ship in a release it cannot
      name has no version because the work is not done, which is what
      the rule is for.

1.98  2026-08-15

    - TKT-129 (police half): police watches the board with the code that
      is installed, not the code it started with. Reported by the owner:
      "When installed new Tira in new version. Dashboard and police
      won't reload themselves."
    - The machinery has existed since the dashboard needed it and
      nothing in the police loop ever called it, so a police left
      running through a release kept the rulebook it started with -
      rules that shipped since were not evaluated, wording that had been
      corrected was still printed, and it said nothing about either. A
      watcher reading old rules looks exactly like one reading new ones.
    - Measured on this project's own board within the hour: a fix that
      had shipped, passed its gate and reached origin went on being
      contradicted by the police still running the previous version,
      which raised the same violation to CRITICAL seven times.
    - Between rounds, never during a pass, because police writes the
      bridge and the enforcement ledger and a pass cut in half leaves a
      violation counted and unsaid. And once: exec never returns, so in
      a running police this can only happen once by construction, but a
      restarter that does return now ends the watch rather than trying
      again every interval - that is the loop four dashboards spent
      twenty hours in, and a police that has stopped is visible while
      one restarting on a timer is not.
    - The dashboard half is not in this release. It was written down as
      impossible - a served board cannot replace itself - and that was
      reasoned from the failure we hit rather than from the tool. He
      sent me to Starman's documentation: HUP restarts workers
      gracefully while the master keeps the listening socket. Because
      the app is built in-process before Plack::Runner sees it, the
      documented answer is Server::Starter holding the socket, which is
      a dependency and a change to how the board is launched, so it gets
      its own release rather than riding along here.
    - TKT-213: a fold written in the same second as the mark is a fold.
      mt5-ai isolated it by comparing three cards their script had
      handled identically - marked 10:40:20 written 10:40:21 silent,
      marked 10:42:33 written 10:42:34 silent, marked 10:44:32 written
      10:44:32 reported. Only the equal pair fired.
    - Stamps are second-resolution, so a fold written inside the same
      second as the mark cannot be strictly later, and the rule asked
      for strictly later. An agent that marks and folds in one script -
      which is the correct thing to do and exactly what the rule asks
      for - lands in the same second whenever the board is quick. So the
      agents that behave best were the ones most likely to be told they
      did not, and told unfalsifiably: the message says nothing was
      written down while the thing is written down.
    - What it gives up is recorded rather than glossed: a detail written
      in the same second BEFORE the mark now reads as a fold. That needs
      an agent to have just written a detail field, which is close to
      folding anyway, and the alternative is accusing the diligent.
    - A card marked ok with nothing written into it is still reported,
      which is the assertion that matters. The first attempt at this fix
      made the rule stop firing altogether and the test caught it.
    - TKT-206: card-sandbox-missing can be watched passing. There was no
      defect - t/195 builds developer-dashboard's exact arrangement and
      every assertion passes on the shipped code, so DD-532 is a rename:
      their branch is dd-532 and the card is DD-532, and the check wants
      a branch named exactly after the card, which the violation has
      said in those words since 1.73.
    - What was missing is the half they identified themselves. Five
      cards cleared that rule on their board in one morning and every
      one cleared by leaving the column, which the --enter scope makes
      it stop applying to - so nobody had ever observed it pass. A rule
      that can only be seen to stop applying is one nobody can trust,
      whatever the verdict on any single card, and that observation is
      now a standing test.

1.97  2026-08-15

    - TKT-208: the bridge prints UTF-8 rather than whatever Perl
      guesses. He hit it while tailing the bridge - "Wide character in
      print at .../lib/Tira/CLI.pm line 1932" - which is the replay
      handing decoded characters straight to a handle that is
      deliberately :raw.
    - Standard output is raw on purpose: Perl's text layer on Windows
      rewrites every newline, so the bytes leaving the process would not
      be the bytes Tira produced, and Tira compares output bytes in its
      own cache. Everything that formats output therefore encodes
      itself, through _utf8_bytes. Three prints did not - the bridge
      replay, the bridge follow loop, and the --refs-only listing.
    - The warning is the milder half. Above U+00FF Perl warns and then
      writes UTF-8 anyway, so the text arrives intact with a warning
      stitched into whatever is reading. Between U+0080 and U+00FF there
      is no warning at all: Perl writes a single latin-1 byte, which is
      not valid UTF-8. A card title carrying an accented name or a
      multiplication sign written as U+00D7 put exactly the byte
      tira.doctor exists to repair into the channel that reports it.
    - All three now encode like every other output path. The follow loop
      matters as much as the replay: fixing the first screen and leaving
      every line after it wrong is the worse half, because a tail is
      what an agent leaves running.
    - TKT-205: a backup of a board nobody touched says nothing changed,
      and stops carrying somebody's sign-in. He noticed tira.backup
      reporting changed: 1 on runs seconds apart with nothing touched,
      and guessed the backup wrote a stamp that guaranteed the next one
      had work to do. Nearly right: nothing the backup writes causes it -
      a session file under the store is rewritten whenever anybody uses
      the board, reading included.
    - The consequence he named is the small one: "is this board already
      backed up" had no cheap answer. The larger one is what a session
      is. It is the server side of a sign-in, and it was being committed
      into the backup history for ever. The manual already made this
      argument about the only other exclusion - a restored lock is
      somebody else's half-finished write - and a restored session is
      somebody else's sign-in, which is worse.
    - The exclusion is checked on every backup rather than written once
      when the store is made. It used to be written only at creation, so
      a line added there would have fixed new boards and left every
      board that already exists tracking sessions for ever - and every
      board that exists today was made before this.
    - A board that already tracked them is told to stop, in the index
      only. What it has already committed stays committed: rewriting the
      history of a backup is a worse thing to own than the tidiness it
      would buy, and the documentation says so rather than implying a
      backup gets scrubbed.
    - TKT-202: a card being worked anywhere counts as a card being
      worked. Whether anything is in progress - which work-without-card
      rests on - counted only cards in the single column named by the
      in-progress role. This project's own board declares
      in-progress=implement and has five columns work happens in, so
      tests-red, verify, document and push read as nobody working.
    - Measured here rather than reasoned about: VIO-0013 reached
      CRITICAL and was said five times while a card sat in verify,
      assigned, with its suite running and the tree changing under it
      because that is what working on a card looks like.
    - It now asks the board where work happens, the same question
      card-unassigned and priority-skipped ask: not protected, and not
      marked terminal, with done as the fallback for a board that has
      marked nothing. A board that declares no roles is unchanged.
    - The half that mattered more than the fix is that backlog, the
      ending column and discard still do not count. A rule that never
      fires would have traded a false alarm for a silent one, which is
      the worse trade.
    - The documentation half, which is why this was hard to see. The
      reference said "the vocabulary is the project's own; Tira matches
      a role without needing to understand it" while two names were
      understood and hard-coded. They behaved differently: done says out
      loud when it cannot find its role, in-progress said nothing - so a
      board using its own word for it fell through to the wide reading
      and was watched correctly by accident, while a board using Tira's
      word was watched in one column out of five. Following the
      documentation gave the right behaviour. Now only done is read by
      Tira itself, and the reference says so.

1.96  2026-08-15

    - TKT-204: a board that says where its work lives is backed up
      where it lives. developer-dashboard reported board-unbacked
      raised at 07:55 and escalated twice while the board was backed up
      three times in between, against a seven-day age.
    - The cause was one variable answering two questions. _police_world
      resolves the repository a project declared, falling back to the
      board directory, and asked everything with it. Branches, work
      trees, unpushed commits and whether the tree is changing all want
      the repository. The backup lookup wants the board: the store is
      <board>/.tira, which is where tira.backup writes and the only
      place it ever writes, and where restore and export read it from.
    - So a board that had declared a repository had its backups looked
      for inside the code, found none, and was told it had never been
      backed up - permanently, whatever anybody did about it. A board
      that declared nothing had the two directories in the same place
      and worked, which is why this project's own board settled the
      same rule at 07:28 that morning while his could not.
    - That variable arrived in 1.91 with TKT-178, which taught police to
      read a declared repository so card-sandbox-missing would stop
      reporting branches that plainly existed. It fixed exactly the
      boards it broke, which is why the report could describe one rule
      settling promptly in the same breath as the other never settling.
    - His own guess is worth recording as wrong, because it was the
      reasonable one: that police gathers the machine facts once rather
      than per round. It gathers them every round, and the loop says so.
      Acting on the guess would have produced a fix for something that
      was already right.
