1.90  2026-08-15

    - TKT-177: board-still. His request - if nothing has moved for a
      period the agent sets, police asks on the bridge why. It is the one
      rule here that is not about a card: every other rule needs
      something to attach itself to, so a board where every card sits in
      the backlog and none has moved for a day looks, to all of them,
      exactly like a board with nothing wrong.
    - It says when the board last moved and how long ago, because "over
      the limit" alone cannot tell a quiet afternoon from a board
      abandoned on Friday. Everything counts as movement - a card
      created, a field written, a comment, an answer, a checklist tick, a
      discard, a column changed - and it is the newest of those anywhere
      on the board, so one card being worked keeps a board of forgotten
      ones quiet.
    - The age is required with no default: an hour of quiet is nothing on
      a research board and a working day is a crisis on a delivery one.
    - The other half of his request needed nothing built. tira.rule.suspend
      already puts one rule down for a while with a reason, has a ceiling
      and comes back by itself, so the message points at it by name and
      the documentation shows the planning-afternoon case.
    - It waited for 1.89. Built on last_updated before a column move
      stamped the card, this rule would have called a board busy with
      moves completely still.
    - Two mutations caught nothing and both were faults in the test
      rather than in the rule. Reversing the sort from newest to oldest
      changed no verdict, because every case had a single card - so the
      choice the whole rule rests on had never been asked, and a
      two-card case was added. And a `next if !defined` guard for the
      empty board turned out to be dead code: _policy_older_than already
      treats a stamp it cannot read as not old. The guard was removed and
      the real reason written down.

1.89  2026-08-15

    - TKT-198: a card whose only change was moving column was invisible
      to every since filter. The column is not a field in the record - it
      is which directory the file sits in - so record_move renamed the
      file, journalled the move, and never rewrote the record. Every
      since filter reads last_updated, which stayed where it was.
    - Measured on both paths. A card created at 09:00 and moved to
      implement at 18:00 reported last_updated 09:00: record.list --since
      12:00 returned nothing, and an incremental export returned nothing.
      The card moved six hours after the threshold and neither saw it.
    - The code and the reference both said this must not happen. The
      comment above _changed_since reads "since-filtering may skip quiet
      records but must never hide one", and the reference promises that a
      poller passing the export envelope's own clock back as its next
      --since "can never miss a change". A poller doing exactly that
      missed every column move.
    - A move now stamps the card. A move to the column a card is already
      in still changes nothing at all, so a command that did nothing
      cannot wake a poller.
    - Found while building board-still for TKT-177, which needed to know
      when a board last did anything: the obvious source was the newest
      last_updated, and a move did not appear in it, so that rule would
      have called a board busy with moves completely still. TKT-177 is
      parked on this rather than built on a stamp that does not move.
    - The test caught itself in the same trap it was written to fix. Its
      content-hash block asserted that a move leaves the hash alone, and
      passed - because content_hash is only computed when a field
      selection asks for it, and record_show without one returns undef,
      so undef equalled undef. The real contract is the opposite: CA05
      covers the computed column, so a move alters the hash. Corrected,
      and proved by dropping the column from the hash and watching it
      fail. Written an hour after TKT-196, which was about exactly that.

1.88  2026-08-15

    - TKT-175: discard-with-open-questions. His request. A card could be
      set aside while it still carried questions nobody had answered, and
      nothing said so - the questions went with the card, not answered,
      not withdrawn, not asked anywhere else, and the decision they were
      waiting on was never made.
    - The rule reports a card in Discard that still has an unanswered
      question, names the questions, and says what to do: decide whether
      each still matters, ask the ones that do on the card they belong to
      now, and discard them here.
    - Police asks and moves nothing. Whether a question still matters is
      a judgement about the work, and a rule that carried questions
      between cards on its own would be making that judgement by machine,
      where a wrong guess is indistinguishable from a decision somebody
      made.
    - There is no command that moves a question and the message does not
      pretend otherwise - asking it where it belongs and discarding it
      here is the move. A message naming a command nobody can run is
      worse than one that explains itself.
    - Three things are not this rule: a question answered before the card
      was set aside, one withdrawn with question.discard, and an
      unanswered question on a live card, which belongs to
      question-unanswered. Each is asserted rather than assumed.
    - Documented as he asked - in the rules table, in a section of its
      own, and with the onboarding line that declares it beside the five
      rules watching a question while its card is alive. Those five cover
      a question while the card lives; this covers the one moment they
      can all be escaped at once.

1.87  2026-08-15

    - TKT-184: the release gate proves the commits being pushed rather
      than the desk they were written on. The pre-push suite ran against
      the working directory, so anything lying there judged the release -
      and this repository's standing state is that the next card's
      failing test already exists, because the process is write the
      failing test first and push each card as it finishes.
    - It refused three pushes for reasons that were not about them, and
      the third lost 1.85 outright: t/86 requires every declared rule to
      fire, and the next card had already added one to the catalogue that
      was not yet implemented. The commit was green when it was made and
      green again twenty minutes later; only the tree it was judged
      against was ever wrong. A card sat in done carrying a version
      nobody could install until it was corrected by hand.
    - The gate now checks out HEAD into a throwaway work tree, mounts it
      over the path the container expects, and removes it however the run
      ends - the failure paths being the common ones.
    - Stricter than before rather than looser. A commit that passes only
      because of an uncommitted file - a helper never added, a module
      still unstaged - now fails at the gate instead of failing for
      everybody else.
    - Proved side by side at one moment, with a red test on the desk and
      not in the commit: the old way FAIL, the new way PASS with the red
      test simply absent from the release. Then with that same test
      committed: FAIL again, so a genuinely broken commit is still
      refused.

1.86  2026-08-15

    - TKT-171: priority-skipped. A card being worked while a
      higher-priority card of the same kind sits untouched where it was
      raised is reported, naming both and the priority passed over.
    - He caught this by eye - "you randomly pick and work on them
      disregard the card prioity" - and the repair at the time was a
      sentence in a document. It is worth a rule for a sharper reason
      than forgetfulness: the agent raises its own cards and sets their
      priority, so working the highest first is a weak promise when the
      same party decides what is highest. What a rule can watch is the
      part that cannot be marked as its own homework.
    - A higher card waiting on an unanswered question is parked rather
      than skipped, and is reported again the moment the answer arrives.
      Cards of different kinds are not compared, because an epic sits
      where it was raised for as long as its tickets take. A card with no
      priority is unassessed rather than urgent. No age: being passed
      over does not ripen, and an age is refused at declaration.
    - It could not be written until TKT-186 settled which end of the
      scale is urgent. The first draft had it inverted and asserted a
      violation on work taken in the correct order - a guard that would
      have enforced the exact opposite of what was asked for.
    - Four mutations, each caught by a named assertion: comparing the
      wrong way round, forgetting that a parked card is not skipped,
      comparing across kinds, and dropping the check that keeps an unset
      priority out of the comparison. That last one caught nothing at
      first, because the only case with no priorities had none on either
      side and was stopped by a different guard - so the case was added,
      and with it an assertion that the pass reads no undefined value,
      which is the only thing that check actually does.

1.85  2026-08-15

    - TKT-197: five options that work on project.new and were documented
      only for project.update. The CLI's own ownership guard says
      reminder settings belong to project.update, project.new and
      onboard; SKILLS.md documented --notify-after, --collector, --agent,
      --session and --heartbeat against project.update alone, and
      project.new's signature listed none of them.
    - All five work there. Measured before anything was written:
      project.new carrying agent, session, collector, heartbeat and
      notify-after stores every one, project.show reads them back, and
      they are in project.yml. Nothing was broken - a real capability was
      simply unfindable, against a command whose reason for existing is
      doing the whole of onboarding in a single call.
    - project.update was documented twice in the same file, in two
      different shapes, and neither list was complete: a bullet carrying
      the name and dashboard options, and a fenced block carrying the
      reminder settings. The bullet now carries all of them.
    - The guard and the signatures are checked against each other rather
      than against a third list somebody would have to maintain, so a
      command added to the guard fails until it is written down.
    - Found by a hunt probing for the shape of TKT-182 - an option a
      command is validated as allowed to carry and then drops. That probe
      came back clean: all seven static suspects were bulk-copied
      settings that do work, each confirmed by running it. This was the
      same drift running the other way.

1.84  2026-08-15

    - TKT-186: the priority scale says which end is urgent. Tira runs 1
      to 5 with 5 the most urgent, which is the opposite of the P1
      convention most trackers use - and nothing anywhere said so. The
      validator gave only the range, neither document mentioned priority,
      and the direction existed only in a JavaScript label map and a sort
      inside the browser dashboard.
    - So it was gettable wrong in silence, and it was got wrong for a
      whole session: every card raised with --priority 1 meaning "top"
      was recorded as Low and sorted to the bottom of the column he was
      reading. He corrected two of them through the dashboard without
      saying anything - TKT-177 from 1 to 5 within two minutes, TKT-166
      from 2 to 5 within sixteen - and nothing would have told the agent.
    - The refusal for an out-of-range value now states the direction,
      because that is where somebody who typed the wrong number meets the
      scale without reading documentation. SKILLS.md and the command
      reference state it too; the reference had not mentioned priority at
      all.
    - Three copies of one decision cannot be reduced to one - a browser
      cannot read a Perl string and a refusal cannot be a JavaScript
      object - so they are held against each other instead. Flipping the
      sort, flipping the labels, or dropping the sentence from either the
      refusal or the reference each fails a named assertion.
    - The direction itself is not changed and no card is renumbered. A
      board that upgrades finds its priorities meaning exactly what they
      meant before.
    - TKT-171 was blocked on this. Its first draft was written with the
      scale inverted, which would have shipped a rule enforcing the exact
      opposite of the order it was asked for, while looking like a guard.

1.83  2026-08-15

    - TKT-196: assertions that could not fail, named as though they
      could. Ten across the suite matched qr/\S/ - any non-empty text.
      Most were honest preconditions and said so in their own names.
      t/95-windows-replace.t's "and says why" was not: it checked that
      an error message was non-empty while claiming to check that it
      explained itself.
    - Proved by mutation rather than by argument. Stripping the reason
      out of the message - die "Cannot replace $path" instead of "Cannot
      replace $path: $error" - left "ok 10 - and says why" passing and
      the whole file green. The code was right all along; what was wrong
      was a guard that would not have noticed if it stopped being right.
    - Found by the hourly hunt. One of the ten had been written an hour
      earlier, widening an assertion after the failure it described
      changed shape - which is how they get in: a specific pattern stops
      matching and the quickest repair is to stop matching anything.
    - Four now pin what their names promise: the replace error names the
      reason the system gave, the policy listing names both policies it
      claims to print, the served police log is compared word for word
      against what was recorded, and the unreadable reason names the kind
      of failure. Six that legitimately only need non-empty say so with a
      named marker beside them, the way t/147 and t/149 already mark
      their exceptions.
    - The guard that keeps it that way twice reported itself: first its
      own header, which quotes the assertion that prompted it, and then
      the two examples it carries to show it can tell a marked line from
      an unmarked one. Both are excluded by shape rather than by name.

1.82  2026-08-15

    - TKT-166: the agent is told when Tira has been upgraded under it.
      His request. When a new version is installed the owner gets the
      setup prompt in his own terminal and the agent gets nothing -
      though the agent is the party that has to read what changed, learn
      the commands that are new, and declare the rules that arrived with
      them. A rule nobody has declared is silent in exactly the way a
      rule being obeyed is, so an upgrade nobody mentions leaves a board
      quietly running an older rulebook.
    - Police now says it on the bridge, addressed to the board's agent
      rather than to anyone, naming the version and the three commands
      that act on it: tira.changes, tira.usage and
      tira.policy.undeclared. The last of those is the "compare them and
      see any gaps" half of his message, which has had a command of its
      own since 1.66 - so nobody is asked to diff two lists by eye.
    - Written first on the bridge, because it changes how the lines under
      it should be read: a rule that arrived with this version is one the
      board has not declared yet, and its absence below is not evidence
      of anything.
    - Once per version rather than once per start. Police restarts in
      order to pick a new version up, so a line on every start would
      arrive on a loop for as long as nobody upgraded again.
    - A version going backwards counts as a change and is said the same
      way. The first test asserted silence there, which would have left
      an agent working from a rulebook the installed Tira no longer has.

1.81  2026-08-14

    - TKT-193: two violations nobody could put down. card-unreadable
      shipped in 1.78 and card-damaged in 1.80, and neither was in the
      rule catalogue - so rule.suspend and policy.decline both refused
      them with "Unknown policy rule", while every other rule on the
      board accepted both. A board with permanently damaged files had a
      violation it could not stop by any means.
    - Found by the hourly hunt, probing the mirror of a class this
      project keeps finding: not a check that never fires, but one that
      fires and cannot be stopped. Two copies of one decision had
      drifted - what counts as a rule. The catalogue held twenty-six,
      police raised twenty-eight, and the two extra were pushed straight
      into the pass rather than declared, so everything reading the
      catalogue was blind to them.
    - There was a second half, and it is the one a careless fix would
      have missed: those two are assembled in police_pass rather than
      reported through the rule loop, so they never met the suspension
      check at all. Teaching the command to accept the name without
      teaching the pass to honour it would have given an agent a
      suspension that reported success and changed nothing.
    - Both can now be put down for a while with a reason and declined
      outright, and the pass honours both. Neither is declarable, because
      there is nothing to configure, and neither appears among the rules
      a board has not decided about. A board that has answered neither is
      still told when a card is damaged - silence about a corrupt record
      is the fault this whole thread began with.
    - Proved by breaking each thing it protects: accepting the
      suspension without honouring it, ignoring a decline, and making
      damage quiet by default, each caught by a named assertion. The
      first attempt at those mutations ran without installing
      dependencies, so a compile failure read as a caught mutation; a
      later one hit the wrong of two identical lines and caught nothing.
      Both runs were discarded and the substitutions are verified before
      the run now.

1.80  2026-08-14

    - TKT-191: a card damaged by one byte is read rather than set aside.
      1.78 stopped a malformed byte silencing a whole board by skipping
      the card it could not decode; he read that and said the obvious
      thing back - do not skip it, read it. He is right, and the skip was
      the weaker answer by this project's own standard: a skipped card is
      a card nobody is checking, which is the disease that release was
      about, reproduced at a smaller scale.
    - His diagnosis was nearly exact. Perl opens the file perfectly well;
      it is the strict UTF-8 decode that treats a malformed byte as
      fatal. Decoding with substitution returns the whole entry - proved
      on his own damaged line before any of this was written: field,
      reference, and the text on both sides of the byte all came back.
    - He offered to bring in Python or C to clean the file. Not needed,
      and the wrong shape: a second language added to work around a
      decoding flag.
    - So history is read with substitution, every rule judges the card
      normally, and the damage is still reported once - as `card-damaged`
      rather than `card-unreadable`, because a file to clean at leisure
      and a card nobody is checking call for opposite things from
      whoever is reading. Nothing rewrites the file: history is the
      permanent record, and a program that edits it unattended is a worse
      problem than the one it solves.
    - Measured on a copy of the reporting board: 447 violations before,
      452 after, with the two formerly skipped cards now checked and
      carrying three real violations that nothing had ever reported.
      Nothing is listed unreadable any more, and both files still hold
      their original bytes.

1.79  2026-08-14

    - TKT-189: a police pass that crashed no longer announces every open
      violation as fixed. An empty violation list means "everything that
      was open is now resolved", and a pass that dies hands back an empty
      list - so the last thing the dying pass on the reporting board ever
      said was "SETTLED VIO-0018 answer-ok-not-folded no longer applies
      here, fix: nothing - this one is over", nine seconds after the rule
      that killed it was declared.
    - Still live in 1.78, which fixed the silence and left this. It is
      worse than the silence: a clean board and a dead one at least look
      the same, while "this one is over" is a statement that work was
      done, about work nobody did.
    - A failed pass now settles nothing. What was open stays open with
      the same number, and is not announced as having returned, because
      it never went away. A healthy pass settles what has genuinely
      stopped being true, exactly as before.

    - TKT-190: the notice naming a card police cannot read is on the
      quiet ladder with everything else. It was printed to the owner's
      terminal on every pass - and the byte that causes it sits in a file
      six days old that nobody is going to repair, so that was a line
      every thirty seconds for the rest of the board's life. He read it
      back within the hour: "I thought you have a workaround".
    - The workaround was working; the telling of it was the repetition a
      warning system dies of, introduced by the fix for a silence in the
      same release. Nothing about it was special enough to sit outside
      the ledger, and it no longer does: it gets a number, a count of
      tellings, one escalation, and a settlement if the card ever becomes
      readable again.

1.78  2026-08-14
    - TKT-188: the documented-examples check no longer creates a board
      where the live board lives. It ran every example from whatever
      directory it was started in, which in the push gate is the root of
      this repository. Not every command takes --project -
      tira.project.create is documented as creating a project in the
      current directory and does exactly that with a --project it has no
      use for - so the check created a board at the repository root.
    - It never did damage, and the reason is not reassuring: the real
      board was already there, so the command refused. That directory is
      the one destroyed on 2026-08-11 by test containers running against
      it, with a morning of cards lost and nothing to restore from.
    - Found by the guard written after that day. The new test above is
      the first to invoke this tool from inside the suite; the testing
      compose file masks the board directory with an empty tmpfs, so the
      write landed in memory and 74-production-board.t failed at once,
      saying a test run could reach a project at the repository root. A
      structural repair caught this three months later with nobody
      watching for it.
    - The examples now run from the scratch board they were given, and a
      test asserts the tool creates nothing in the directory it was
      started from.
    - TKT-185: a changelog that describes an error no longer reads as
      one. The documented-examples check scans a command's whole output
      for error text, and tira.changes prints the changelog - which
      describes a bug whose symptom was "Command project.show not found
      in skill tira". So printing it contained that sentence and the push
      gate refused the release, twice, on the grounds that a shipped
      command does not exist.
    - Third appearance of one shape, after a test that could fail by
      chance and a gate that judges uncommitted work: a release refused
      for a reason that is not about the release. It would have got worse
      on its own, because every entry written about an error message adds
      another copy of that message to what the changelog prints.
    - Commands that recite a document - changes, skills, usage, policies
      - are now judged by whether they succeeded rather than by what the
      document says. That is stricter than the scan, not looser: a
      document that fails to print now fails instead of passing quietly.
      Every other command is still read for the failures that mean the
      documentation describes something Tira will not do.
    - The first attempt named the new list DOCUMENTS, which already named
      the list of files to scan. The shadow made the check find zero
      examples, report them all understood and exit 0 - a check passing
      by having tried nothing, which is the fault it exists to prevent
      one level down. Its guard asserts a count as well as a status.
    - TKT-180: one card nobody can read no longer silences the whole
      board. He reported that answering a question raised a bridge note
      on one of his boards and not on the other, with the same rule
      declared on both. It was not the answer rule: police was reporting
      zero violations across 27 declared policies and 359 cards, saying
      it was watching, and exiting 0 - so an unpoliced board and a clean
      one produced identical output.
    - The cause was one byte. Two history files held a raw 0xD7 where a
      multiplication sign was meant, written as latin-1 rather than
      UTF-8. History is decoded as strict UTF-8, so the read died inside
      the rule loop and took every rule with it. Only rules that read a
      journal reach it - conversation-not-folded, through
      _last_card_change, and column-skipped - which is why one board was
      affected and the other was not.
    - Two real violations had been hidden that way for as long as the
      byte had been there.
    - The history read is now guarded in one place both rules share. The
      third reader in the same file, _policy_last_detail_change, had
      always guarded its own; this is that decision written once instead
      of three times with two copies drifting.
    - A card that cannot be read is named rather than skipped, because
      skipping quietly is the same fault one level down: an unreadable
      card would look exactly like a card with nothing wrong. It is
      returned as `unreadable` and said out loud in the owner's terminal.
    - An unreadable journal is not an unwritten card, so neither rule
      accuses it of anything on the strength of a fault of their own.
    - A pass that failed outright can no longer be presented as a clean
      pass. The error field it already had was read by nothing: the
      bridge is written from the violations and the terminal from
      escalations, so a failure landing in neither was a failure nobody
      heard about.
    - Verified against a copy of the reporting board with its own 27
      policies restored from its git: 0 violations before, 444 after,
      both damaged cards named with what was wrong. His files were not
      touched - the fix is in Tira, so the next board with a stray byte
      is told rather than silenced.

1.77  2026-08-14
    - TKT-176: a test that could fail by chance no longer can. The login
      test asserted that eight random hex tokens joined together contain
      none of "michael", "hunter2" or "2026" - and a year is four hex
      characters, which appear by chance about once in every hundred and
      forty runs. It failed exactly that way in a push gate on a release
      that touched nothing near it, which is the worst thing a check can
      do: refuse a release for a reason that is not about the release.
    - It also proved less than it looked like it proved. A token that
      genuinely was the clock would have passed whenever the year
      appeared in another form. What refuses a derived token is that
      eight logins by the same person at the same fixed instant produce
      eight different tokens, and that assertion is now shown catching a
      token built from the person and the moment, rather than being
      trusted because it has never failed.
    - TKT-174: the gate survives an install in every step rather than the
      one that failed first. TKT-168 hardened the board audit, and the
      very next push failed the same way one step earlier: "backing up
      the board / Command project.show not found in skill tira".
      board-backup makes four calls and had none of the protection that
      had just been added beside it.
    - That is this project's own recurring fault, committed an hour after
      writing about it: the fix went where the failure appeared rather
      than where the class lives. Running the backup by hand immediately
      afterwards wrote 179 records, the same signature as the two audit
      failures before it.
    - Both tools call through one small script now. They are shell and
      python and cannot share a function, so they share a file - one
      decision in one place rather than the same decision written twice
      in two dialects that can drift. A tool added tomorrow inherits it
      instead of learning this the way board-backup just did.
    - Only the failure that means an install is waited on. Anything else
      stops the gate at once and is passed on exactly as the command said
      it, and a call that never resolves fails saying how many attempts
      were made.

1.76  2026-08-14
    - TKT-172: tira.changes can no longer exit zero having printed
      nothing. He asked why it returned 0 bytes; zen-framework reported
      the same thing independently the same minute, with the measurement
      that mattered - nothing from inside their project directory, the
      whole file from /tmp, while tira.skills, tira.usage and
      tira.policies were all fine from the same place.
    - Not reproducible here: it prints the changelog from a fresh Tira
      project, from this skill's own directory, from /tmp, in every
      output format. Their files are not this agent's to read, so what is
      fixed is not the cause but the silence.
    - An empty or blank changelog now fails and names the file it read.
      That is what tells the next reporter which copy of the skill
      resolved for them, which is the question nobody could answer from a
      silent exit. A real changelog prints byte for byte as before, and a
      missing one fails as loudly as it always did.
    - Their framing is this project's oldest lesson and is why it is a
      P1: an instrument quiet because it is broken looks exactly like one
      quiet because there is nothing to say. A script cannot tell those
      apart, and one of theirs reads this command hourly to record which
      version it audited against - while the command exists so a project
      that reported a bug can see the fix land, which makes silence read
      as nothing having changed since they reported it.

1.75  2026-08-14
    - TKT-169: the card dialog's logs are readable on a phone. He sent a
      photograph of "What police has said" on his own phone reading "was
      answe / red and never marke / d" - one or two letters a line - and
      a second of the work log, rows hundreds of pixels tall with nothing
      visible in them.
    - One cause, two faces. Both sections lay an entry out as a grid of
      fixed columns: the police log takes 11rem and 7rem before the
      detail, which leaves about three on a phone, and the card sets
      overflow-wrap to anywhere, so three rems is a column of single
      letters. The work log takes 11rem, 6rem and 7rem - twenty-four rems
      of fixed columns on a twenty-two rem screen - so its value is
      pushed outside the card and the rows are tall but look empty.
    - Both stack on a narrow screen now and break at word boundaries,
      and the wide layout is untouched.
    - The browser test that already covered this section passed
      throughout, because it asks whether the content is there and a
      column of single letters contains everything. It measures the
      detail now: with the fix taken out it reports "the police log
      detail is 12px wide on a phone", which is his photograph in a
      number.
    - TKT-168: the board audit survives the skill being installed
      underneath it. The gate failed twice reporting a shipped command as
      unknown - tira.epic.show once, tira.ticket.list the next push -
      because the installed directory was being rewritten inside the
      gate's window and the dispatcher resolves a command by finding its
      entrypoint. Only that failure is waited on, anything else still
      stops the gate at once, and a command that never resolves fails it
      saying how many times it tried.

1.74  2026-08-14
    - TKT-167: two commands no longer claim to do the same specific
      thing. tira.backup.export and tira.backup.import both described
      themselves as "Put the board back to its last backup", which is
      what tira.backup.restore does and what neither of them does -
      export writes a bundle out, import reads one into a board that has
      none. Both also carried the same half-finished sentence. Found by
      the hourly bug hunt.
    - A wrong-action risk rather than an untidy comment: somebody reading
      that on the export entrypoint could run export believing it
      restores, and export is the one that writes a file, so the mistake
      stays quiet until the day they need the restore they thought they
      had.
    - The hunt's first probe passed both files. It asserted that a NAME
      line contains the command it resolves to, and both do - the fault
      is in the summary beside it, and the second probe, summaries used
      by more than one entrypoint, is what found it. That shape is now
      the guard.
    - 110 of the 130 entrypoints share the dispatcher's own summary,
      because those files really are copies of it, and that is allowed by
      name rather than by a list of files - an exception written as a
      list is one somebody widens. Giving each of the 110 its own
      sentence is worth doing and is not this card; since 1.70 the
      command reference names all 141 with their synopses, which is where
      a reader is sent.

1.73  2026-08-14
    - TKT-161: card-sandbox-missing says what it asked the machine.
      developer-dashboard read "missing branch and the work tree it
      records, /home/mv/dd-worktree-sandbox/dd-532, which is not there
      for DD-532" while the directory, the work tree and the branch all
      existed - and the message gave them nothing to check it against.
    - Their hypothesis was right about the branch and they were careful
      to call it a hypothesis: the check wants a branch named exactly
      after the card, a reference is upper case by construction, and git
      branches are conventionally lower case, so on any project following
      that convention it can never match. The violation now names the
      branch it wanted and, where one differs only in case, says which -
      an hour of guessing becomes a rename.
    - It no longer says a branch is not there when it is there under
      another case. That sentence was simply false, and a rule that reads
      the machine cannot afford one.
    - The work tree half says how many the machine reported. None is a
      different fault from one being gone: police pointed at a repository
      that does not hold the work trees reports an empty list, and that
      read exactly like a tree somebody had deleted.

1.72  2026-08-14
    - TKT-160: a violation says what would clear it. answer-ok-not-folded
      reported an answer marked ok with "nothing written down" and never
      said where. A project wrote the whole decision into a comment - the
      question, the answer and its consequence - was told again on the
      next pass, escalated to a warning, then wrote the same words into a
      field and it settled immediately.
    - The sharper half was the inconsistency: discard-unexplained,
      declared minutes earlier on the same board, is cleared by a comment
      alone. Two rules in one set wanting an explanation in different
      places, and neither saying which, so learning the convention from
      one taught the wrong thing about the other.
    - The difference is deliberate and is now stated rather than
      discovered. A discard reason is a note somebody leaves about work
      that has stopped; a folded answer is content the next agent reads
      off the card, and a comment is not read back that way. Both
      messages name the place, and the guide carries a table of which
      rule wants which.
    - They were explicit that the rule earned its keep: it found seven
      genuine cases they would never have found - decisions both parties
      believed were handled that had stopped existing anywhere a person
      would look. This changes what it says, not what it catches.

1.71  2026-08-14
    - TKT-159: a board can say where its work ends. card-unassigned
