1.67  2026-08-14
    - TKT-162: an answer's follow-through is addressed to whoever asked,
      not whoever answered. Every violation went to the card's holder,
      which is right for the rest of them - one agent per ticket, and a
      bridge that hands every agent every violation is noise by
      construction. It is wrong for the four rules about what happens
      after an answer: reading it, judging it, folding it into the card
      and asking anything further are the agent's work, and the owner's
      part ends when he answers.
    - He read it off his own bridge - "for Michael ... Q-017 was marked ok
      and nothing was folded into the card", escalating to WARNING on the
      second telling - and asked why folding in was for the user when it
      is the agent's job.
    - It stopped the work rather than merely misdirecting it. His words:
      the agent confused, that is for me, and they skip to fold in. The
      agent read the addressee, concluded the folding was the owner's,
      and did not do it - so the rule escalated about work its own
      addressing had told the only capable party to ignore. A violation
      addressed to the wrong party is not a message in the wrong
      envelope; it is an instruction the right party never receives.
    - All four are addressed to the author of the question now:
      answer-waiting, answer-unjudged, answer-ok-not-folded and
      answer-not-ok-no-followup. Nothing is inferred - a question stores
      who asked and an answer stores who answered, separately. A question
      with nobody named on it falls back to the card holder rather than
      being addressed to nobody, and every other rule is unchanged.
    - The other twenty-one were examined. question-unanswered is the only
      one whose actor is the owner rather than the agent, and it is left
      as it is: the agent is who chases an unanswered question, so the
      card holder is the right reader for it.

