1.53  2026-08-14
    - TKT-137: an add that cannot take is refused rather than reported as
      done. Adding bytes whose hash was discarded on a card deduped onto
      the discarded record and returned it verbatim - the original
      timestamp, discarded_at still set, deduped true, exit zero - while
      creating no live attachment.
    - It cost a project ten screenshots. They discarded twenty-two
      believing all had changed, ten had not, and their script counted
      exit codes and reported ten fresh attachments having made none. The
      only repair was to change the bytes: they re-rendered, and for one
      file whose pixels were already correct they did a lossless
      re-encode purely to move the hash.
    - The manual made it worse. "Re-adding identical bytes restores the
      object" is true of remove, which deletes the content, and not of
      discard, which sets a card-scoped stamp that beats deduplication -
      and the two sentences sit close together. Both places now say which
      is which.
    - Refused rather than revived. Reviving is friendlier and discard is
      described as setting aside rather than deleting, so being unable to
      put it back is the surprise; but a refusal cannot lose anything and
      a revive can be built on top of one, where it could not be built on
      top of silence. The stamp stays card-scoped, so the same bytes
      still attach to any other card.

1.52  2026-08-14
    - TKT-138: tira.changes, the fourth documentation command, beside
