      commit whose subject names it; for others it could only be guessed,
      and a guessed reference in the one file whose purpose is
      traceability is worse than an absent one. Only the release being
      made is checked.

1.51  2026-08-14
    - TKT-136: the bridge says when a violation stops being true. Police
      always knew - the ledger moves the violation out of open and records
      the moment it closed - and never told the bridge, so the line that
      raised it stayed in the log with nothing marking it dealt with. An
      agent tailing the bridge, or replaying the backlog after a restart,
      read a demand about a card that had settled it hours before.
    - The settlement carries the number of the line it settles, goes to
      the reader the original was addressed to, and is said once. The
      original line stays where it is: the log is a record, and rewriting
      it would be worse than leaving it.
    - Reported as discarded cards inheriting every --enter requirement.
      That mechanism does not exist - entering a column is an exact match
      and has been since police was first written, and the one place that
      compares positions already excludes discard by name. Built on the
      reporter's own column shape, police reports the card in done and
      says nothing about the card in discard.
    - What they saw was their buffered bridge (TKT-132) replaying its
      whole backlog at once: a pile of old lines about cards that had
      moved on, arriving together, reads exactly like a rule firing
      across discard. Fixing the buffering made this matter more rather
      than less, because now the backlogs get read.

1.50  2026-08-13
    - TKT-132: the bridge writes to a file as it goes. tira.policy.bridge
      printed to a standard output nobody had set to flush line by line,
      so Perl buffered it whenever it was not a terminal. Run the natural
      way for something left going - redirected to a file and tailed -
      zen-framework measured ZERO BYTES IN SIXTY-EIGHT MINUTES while
      violations were being raised and escalating to critical. Under a
      pty the same command replayed the whole backlog at once.
    - The bridge is an agent's only channel for violations, and the
      policies guide says a policy set without the bridge running is
      worse than no policy at all because it looks like cover. That
      sentence came true through a buffer: twenty-one policies declared,
      the owner told the board was covered, and the channel writing into
      four kilobytes nobody would ever read.
    - Every existing test missed it because they read police's own log
      file, and because a process flushes what it holds when it exits.
      The new test reads the file with the bridge still running, and
      kills it rather than letting it exit, for the same reason.
    - TKT-131, and TKT-134 reporting it independently: a card is created
      in the column it was asked for. ticket.create accepted --column,
      understood it, and threw it away - the card landed in backlog, the
      command exited zero, and the whole record was printed as though the
      column had been honoured. Three projects hit it in one evening.
    - An unknown flag is refused loudly here, so an accepted flag reads
      as an honoured one. mt5-ai had adopted --column on create precisely
      to claim cards into a working column before starting, and recorded
      in two cards' own gate records a column those cards had never
      occupied. A remedy that fails silently turns a visible gap into a
      false assurance.
    - Honoured rather than refused, which was the other candidate. The
      rules that ship with Tira say backlog means parked on a condition
      and is not an inbox; forcing every card through it puts every new
      card in the state those rules forbid. A column that does not exist
      is refused, so is creating a card straight into discard, and a
      refused create spends no reference number.
    - TKT-130: a test that advanced its clock by two hours did it by adding two to
      the hour field and taking it modulo twenty-four, so a backup made at
      eleven at night moved the clock to one in the morning of the same
      day - twenty-two hours backwards. The rule it was checking stayed
      correctly quiet and the assertion failed.
    - It passed every run of the day and failed in the push gate at ten at
      night, on a commit that touched nothing near it. For twenty-two
      hours out of twenty-four the arithmetic happened not to wrap and the
      assertion was met by accident; the two hours where it was wrong were
      the only two where the test meant anything.
    - The clock is now advanced by arithmetic on the time rather than on
      the digits - parse, add the seconds, format back - using the
      conversions the engine already has, and there is no wrap to get
      wrong. No other test in the suite moves a clock by editing the text
      of a stamp.

1.49  2026-08-13
    - A card talked about since it was last written down is reported. A
      card gathers its real content in comments - somebody pastes
      evidence, answers a question, corrects an assumption - while its
      own details go on saying what they said when it was raised, so the
      board carries two stories on one card. TKT-099 was the example that
      started it: he pasted five bridge lines as a comment, they were the
      best evidence on the card, and the details did not know until he
      asked twice.
    - conversation-not-folded compares two things the work log already
      records: the newest comment, and the newest change to the card. No
      new field, no command to run, nothing to mark by hand - which is
      his answer, given in his own words when asked what a card records
      to say the folding was done.
    - Any change to the card settles it, including one about something
      else. He was asked about exactly that and accepted it: the
      alternative is a marker somebody has to remember to set, and a
      reminder that can be silenced by forgetting is worse than one an
      unrelated edit clears.
    - A card nobody has commented on is never reported, and the rule can
      be put down for one card while a long conversation is folded in -
      the other half of what he asked for, which shipped in 1.48.

1.48  2026-08-13
    - One rule can be put down for a while, without going deaf to
      everything else. police.suspend quiets police entirely, which is
      right when an agent needs to concentrate and wrong when one rule is
      chasing one card. tira.rule.suspend puts a single rule down for a
      period, board-wide or for named cards.
    - Per card is the grain that matters, and it is his: a card being
      worked hard collects comments faster than anybody can fold them,
      and if the only way through that afternoon is silencing the whole
      bridge then the escape hatch is worse than the noise it escapes.
      Every other rule keeps watching, and the same rule keeps watching
      every other card.
    - The same promises police.suspend already makes, for the same
      reasons: an end that arrives by itself, so there is nothing to
      remember to switch back on, and a reason that cannot be skipped,
      because a silence nobody can account for is worse than the noise it
      replaces. Every putting-down is in the enforcement log with its
      rule, its card, its length and its reason. A rule nobody has heard
      of is refused rather than quietly accepted.
    - Both halves of police honour it. The rules that read the board and
      the six that read the machine each consult the same record, because
      quieting a rule in one and not the other is the two-copies fault
      this codebase has been caught with three times already.

1.47  2026-08-13
    - A successful import writes nothing to the error stream. git prints
      "<file> is okay" when a bundle verifies, and a command that worked
      writing to stderr reads like a warning to whoever is watching.
      --quiet does not silence it; git says it anyway.
    - Two earlier attempts were reverted and this is the third. Reopening
      this process's error stream and putting it back looked equivalent
      and was not: every caller that had redirected it got the real one
      back, and four assertions about refusal messages went silent while
      the refusals themselves kept working. Forking a child that silences
      itself is correct and unmeasurable, because the child execs away
      before any coverage counter is written. The parent now hands the
      child a filehandle for its error stream: nothing here is reopened
      and no Perl runs in the child.
    - The test that proves it had to be rewritten too. Asserting on a
      localised STDERR passed while the noise went to the terminal
      anyway, because a Perl-level redirection does not touch the stream
      a child process inherits - the check passing for the wrong reason,
      which is the fault this project keeps finding in its own checks. It
      captures a real child's stream now, and asserts the other half as
      well: a later command in the same process still has its own.

1.46  2026-08-13
    - A command refuses an option it will not act on. tira.assign.set
      takes --person and also accepted --assignee, then threw it away:
      the card came back with the assignee still empty, the command
      exited zero, and the whole unchanged record was printed as though
      something had happened. On the one command whose job is setting the
      assignee, the obvious flag was the one that silently did nothing -
      and a card sat in implement for an hour with nobody on it before
      the owner noticed and asked whether a ghost was working on it.
    - assign.add and assign.remove are covered too. Fixing only the one
      somebody happened to hit is how two commands that agree today drift
      apart tomorrow, which this codebase has now been caught doing three
      times.
    - Narrow on purpose. There is no per-command list of the options each
      one uses, and inventing one for every command would refuse things
      that work today. What is declared is the set where one option names
      the job another does - the set that misleads, because a wrong name
      that parses looks accepted rather than unknown. --assignee is
      untouched on the record commands, where it is what sets a card's
      assignee.

1.45  2026-08-13
    - The commit gate reads the subject line for the cards a commit is
      against, and treats the body as prose. It took every reference
      anywhere in the message, and this project quotes references
      constantly - that is how a commit explains what it found. A message
      describing a defect that mentioned a finished card was refused for
      working on it, and the way through was to take the evidence out of
      the message, which is the wrong direction: the message is where the
      evidence belongs.
    - Stricter in one direction, deliberately. A card named only in the
      body is now refused, and the refusal says only the first line is
      read. A message that hid its card in prose was always ambiguous;
      making that an error beats guessing.
    - Proved by running the real hook against the real board rather than
      by reading it - the existing test only checked the hook's text. A
      finished card in the subject is refused, no card anywhere is
      refused, and a finished card quoted in the body while the subject
      names live work is allowed.

1.44  2026-08-13
    - A column can be ordered by priority. He sent a screenshot of the two
      buttons the board had and asked for one more: priority is the field
      the whole board is arranged around, and it was the one thing a
      column could not be ordered by, so a full column had to be read card
      by card to find what mattered most.
    - Highest first, because the question a column answers is what to pick
      up next. A card nobody has prioritised goes last and says so in the
      markup rather than pretending to a number - unassessed is not the
      same as lowest.
    - The priority travels in the refresh payload as well as the first
      render, so the ordering survives the board rebuilding every card a
      minute later. A sort that works until the first refresh is the shape
      of defect this project keeps finding: right on the path anybody
      checks, wrong on the one nobody does.

1.43  2026-08-13
    - answer-unjudged takes an optional second age, counted from when the
      answer was read. It waited the same age for an answer nobody had
      opened and one read ten minutes ago, though the record knows the
      difference - so an agent that read four answers, folded all four
      into the card and left the marks off had two hours before anything
      said so. That happened, and he asked for the sharpening.
    - One rule with two ages rather than a second rule meaning almost the
      same thing, because two rules firing on nearly the same condition
      is how a bridge becomes noise. The second age is optional: a policy
      with one age behaves exactly as it always has, so nothing changes
      meaning on upgrade.
    - Reading it again buys no further grace - reading is what records
      the read, so a rule that reset on it would be silenced by the act
      it is chasing somebody past. A judged answer is chased by neither
      clock. A read age longer than the age it sits inside is refused
      rather than ignored, because a second grace that outlasts the first
      says nothing and would be believed.

1.42  2026-08-13
    - The card dialog shows what police has said about the card. Every
      card carries a per-card enforcement log, written by police and by
      nobody else; the dialog showed the card's sections, its work log
      and its questions, and not that. So the one place a person opens to
      see everything about a card was missing it - and worse than usually,
      because that log exists precisely so what police said survives the
      bridge scrolling past. The surviving copy was readable only by an
      agent running a command.
    - It offers nothing to change. Police writes that log and nobody else
      may, which is why there is no command to add an entry either, and a
      button on the page would have been the way around that. A card
      police has never mentioned shows no section rather than an empty
      heading, because a heading on every card is how a section teaches
      people to skip past it.
    - Read when the card opens rather than when a section is expanded,
      unlike the work log: there is at most one line per thing police has
      said, and the section has to know whether it has anything before it
      decides whether to appear.
    - Proved in a browser, and that mattered. The dialog is filled in
      three places - opening a card, the detail fetch, and the refresh -
      and the first attempt added the section to one of them. Nothing in
      the markup would have caught it: the section was present and simply
      never asked to fill itself. The suite now asserts all three.
    - answer-waiting no longer chases an answer that has already been
      judged. He asked why police had not picked up any answered
      questions; the rule was simply never declared on this board, which
      was my omission. Declaring it reported eleven answers, and every
      one of them was marked ok days earlier - what they lacked was a
      read, because marking an answer does not record one. Nobody can
      judge an answer they have not read, so a mark settles this as
      surely as a read does. Without it the rule chases every settled
      answer a board has ever had, for ever, which is the nagging the
      quiet ladder was built to end.
    - The check that every shipped entrypoint compiles no longer runs the
      commands it finds. It ran each named command to see whether
      anything handled it, which was wrong twice over: it took over six
      minutes under the coverage collector against four seconds now, and
      it ran real commands against whatever board the working directory
      resolved to - for tira.backup that means committing it. A test that
      asks "is anybody listening" must not also be heard. The name is
      checked against the dispatcher's source instead, which is textual
      and says so.

1.41  2026-08-13
    - The read of the enforcement log is named for what it reads.
      tira.police runs the owner's watching loop; tira.police.log read
      what that loop had written and changed nothing. They shared a
      prefix and nothing else, and he sent the transcript of another
      project's agent working that out over three corrections - wrong in
      both directions on the way, by its own summary. An agent told
      "police is mine" gives up the read too, which is the honest reading
      of the name, and stops seeing every suspension and escalation ever
      recorded. It fails silently: a read nobody makes and a log with
      nothing in it look exactly the same.
    - It is tira.policy.bridge.logs now, beside tira.policy.bridge, which
      that agent had already been told was its own. tira.police is left
      meaning one thing. The old name still answers, so no board breaks
      on upgrade, and the documents still mention it because somebody
      reading an existing script will meet it - it is simply not the name
      the reference teaches.
    - police.suspend is decided and kept rather than overlooked. It has
      the same prefix and not the same fault: it is the agent asking
      police for something, so being named after police is accurate.
      Renaming it would have been change for symmetry rather than for
      meaning.
    - And the reference now says whose each command is, in a table. The
      name made the confusion expensive; nothing stating the boundary is
      what made it possible.

1.40  2026-08-13
    - A card being worked says who is working on it. He sent a screenshot
      of a card reading "implement" with the assignee showing a dash:
      "implementing but no assignee? a ghost working on it?" It was true
      of every card moved into a gate that day - the board said work was
      in progress and could not say by whom, for hours, with
      twenty-three rules watching and none of them asking.
    - card-unassigned reports a card that is neither waiting nor finished
      and has nobody on it. It takes no column, and one is refused rather
      than ignored: the board already says which columns are work,
      because every board is created with its backlog and discard columns
      marked protected. So a column added tomorrow is covered the day it
      exists.
    - That is why it is a rule rather than configuration, which was
      checked before a line was written. card-metrics --enter implement
      --require assignee already reported the same thing for one named
      column - and stops covering the board the moment somebody adds
      another, silently, which is the failure this rule exists to avoid.
    - A card waiting in the backlog is not reported, because that is what
      a backlog is; neither is one set aside, nor one that is done. A
      finished card with nobody on it is history, and chasing it would
      mean chasing every card the board has ever finished.

1.39  2026-08-13
    - An agent working on something else can report a fault in Tira.
      Every project on this machine uses it and none of them could:
      reaching the board where Tira is maintained means knowing which
      board that is, which this skill's instructions deliberately do not
      say. So a fault found by the agent best placed to describe it went
      through a human or went nowhere.
    - tira.dev.found.bug_or_improvement carries the destination itself.
      The caller gives what it found and --from, its own project name,
      and is told nothing about where the report went; its own board is
      untouched. The card is raised in the backlog under the maintainer's
      name, because an agent elsewhere is not a member of that board, and
      the origin is a label so the report can be found again and a
      question asked on it reaches whoever raised it.
    - --from is required. A report nobody can go back to is a report
      nobody can answer.
    - A report arrives as an incomplete card, and that is deliberate: the
      board refuses a release while any live card is incomplete, so an
      incoming report has to be triaged before the next one ships. It is
      documented rather than left to be discovered at a push.

1.38  2026-08-13
    - Every script the skill ships is compiled by the suite, and every one
      that names its command is checked to reach something that handles
      it. Both backup entrypoints shipped in 1.37 as Perl syntax errors
      and the suite stayed green: 131 files, 4306 tests and 100 percent
      statement and subroutine coverage on all three modules, while the
      two commands an agent would type did not run at all. Nothing caught
      it because nothing ran them - every test calls the CLI directly and
      exactly one executed an entrypoint, so the code behind the doorway
      was measured to the last statement and the doorway was not measured
      at all.
    - Proved by breaking it both ways it can break. Broken as the bundle
      entrypoints were, the check names the file and fails; pointed at a
      command that does not exist, it names the file and the command and
      fails. The entrypoints are found rather than listed, because a list
      is a second place to remember and forgetting to check is the whole
      fault.

1.37  2026-08-13
    - A backup can leave the machine. The repository a backup lives in
      sits inside the board's own storage, so it survives a bad edit and
      not a lost disk - whatever destroys the board destroys the backup
      with it. tira.backup.export writes a git bundle: one file holding
      the whole history and every attachment, to be kept somewhere the
      board is not. tira.backup.import lays it out again in a folder that
      was not a board, and what arrives is a working board - it issues
      its own references and can be backed up again where it landed.
    - Import takes its folder as given rather than searching upwards for
      one, because importing is how a board comes into existence
      somewhere and searching would find somebody else's board and write
      into it. Importing over a board that already exists replaces it, so
      without --yes it says what is there and does nothing.
    - A bundle claiming a newer schema than this Tira reads is refused,
      and nothing is written when it is. Tira has no migrations and needs
      none in the other direction: an older board reads correctly because
      defaults are applied on read. A newer one holds shapes these
      readers have never seen, and refusing is honest where migrating is
      impossible.
    - Verifying a bundle needs a repository, which the destination is not
      yet, so it is checked in a scratch repository of its own and the
      destination is untouched until the bundle is known to be good.
    - The version gate checks the changelog too. It compared .env against
      the module and the metadata test and never against the top entry in
      this file, so two releases shipped naming themselves one version
      behind and nothing said a word. A changelog that names the wrong
      version is worse than none: it is the file somebody reads to find
      out what they are running.

1.36  2026-08-13
    - A board can be put back. tira.backup.restore returns it to its last
      backup: the cards, the attachments and the reference counters as
      they were, with anything done since removed rather than merged. Two
      things that only showed up by running it. git reset --hard alone is
      not a restore - a card raised since the backup is untracked, so
      reset leaves it where it is and the command reports success with
      the card still there. And the board has to still be a board
      afterwards, which is the check nobody thinks to make: it can be
      added to and backed up again, and the next card takes the reference
      the restore gave back.
    - It is the only command in Tira that can lose work, so without --yes
      it does nothing but print what would be discarded, by name.
      Counted would tell nobody whether it matters. The warning is
      printed rather than thrown, because a structured error carries one
      string and a multi-line warning inside one reaches the terminal as
      literal backslash-n - which is how the one command that can destroy
      a board ends up with a warning nobody reads.
    - Restoring a board that has never been backed up is refused and says
      so, rather than reporting success about nothing. This exists
      because a backup nobody has restored from is a directory.

1.35  2026-08-13
    - (This entry was labelled 1.34 until 1.37. The changelog heading and
      .env had drifted apart because nothing checked them against each
      other, so a release named itself one version behind for two
      releases. The gate now does; see the note under 1.37.)
    - An answer reaches the agent that was waiting for it. He raised it:
      a question gets answered, police is running, and nothing appears on
      the bridge to get the agent's attention. He was right, and the
      reason is that all four rules about questions chase neglect after a
      grace - question-unanswered wants an answer, answer-unjudged fires
      when one has sat unmarked past its age, answer-ok-not-folded when a
      settled answer was never written down, answer-not-ok-no-followup
      when a cross had no follow-up. None of them announced that an
      answer had arrived. On his board, where answer-unjudged is set at
      two hours, an answer produced nothing for two hours and then only a
      complaint that it had not been marked. An agent that stopped to ask
      something stayed stopped.
    - answer-waiting announces it on the next pass and takes no --age. An
      age is refused rather than ignored, because a grace here would only
      be a delay: the agent could not have acted sooner, it did not know.
      A rule may now declare what it cannot accept as well as what it
      cannot work without, since a setting that is accepted, ignored and
      believed is worse than one that is refused.
    - Reading the answer is what stops it, and reading is what records
      it, so there is nothing to dismiss by hand and nothing that goes
      quiet because somebody said it had. An answer reworded after it was
      read is announced again - it is news the agent has not seen - and a
      question set aside is not announced at all.
    - A bridge line describes the board police is watching rather than the
      one it is standing in. Every line carries the way down to its card,
      so the core agent can walk a violation to whoever holds it - and
      that path was looked up on whatever Tira project the process
      happened to be in, because the police command wrote the bridge
      without saying which board it was about. Found while proving the
      rule above end to end: the message was right and its address was
      not, naming a statement of work and an epic that do not exist on
      the board the violation came from. Proved by running the same pass
      from two directories and getting two different answers. It was
      invisible on the one board where it cannot go wrong - this
      repository, where police runs from the project it watches.

1.33  2026-08-13
    - A board can be backed up with a command that ships. board-unbacked
      had been reporting "the board has never been backed up" while Tira
      shipped nothing that could back one up: the backups it read were
      written by a development tool inside Tira's own repository, run by
      its push gate. On that repository the rule worked; on every other
      board it fired, the escalation line pointed at tira.policy.list,
      which backs nothing up, and no command would clear it. A rule that
      can only be obeyed by switching it off teaches whoever reads the
      bridge that some lines are not worth acting on, which is the
      failure police exists to prevent. He read it off his own bridge and
      asked what backup.
    - tira.backup makes a commit in a git repository Tira manages inside
      the board's own storage, beside the project file. It is created the
      first time you back up, so nobody has to run git init to obey a
      rule, and a board that never backs up has none - reading never
      makes one. No remote: a board that lives on a filesystem should not
      need somebody else's machine to be backed up. Attachments are in
      it, because a backup is everything or it is not a backup, and the
      lock file is the one thing left out, because a restored lock is
      somebody else's half-finished write. Backing up an unchanged board
      is not an error - it says nothing had changed and names the backup
      that still stands, since a command that failed on a quiet afternoon
      would teach whoever reads the bridge to ignore it.
    - The commit carries an identity given on the command rather than
      written into the repository, and signing is switched off for it, so
      backing up neither depends on the runner's git being configured nor
      changes what it would do, and a signing prompt cannot hang a
      command police asks for on a schedule. Git is run from the command
      layer: the engine still invokes no shell or external process.
    - board-unbacked reads that repository, so backing a board up is what
      makes it go quiet. It read a directory under the home folder that
      only tools/board-backup ever wrote to, which made the rule
      satisfiable on exactly one board on earth and permanently
      unsatisfiable everywhere else. The old place is still read, so a
      board backed up by the old tool is not suddenly told it never was.
    - And the line names the command that clears it. Every rule about the
      board itself ended in "fix: d2 tira.policy.list", so the loudest
      tone board-unbacked can reach still told whoever read it to go and
      list their policies, which backs nothing up. The fix line was built
      by the same expression written out twice - once for the bridge and
      once for the owner's terminal - which is the shape that has now
      drifted three times in this codebase. It is one place now.
    - The push gate runs documented examples against the code being
      pushed rather than against whatever was installed last. It invoked
      every example through the installed skill, so documentation for a
      command a commit deletes kept passing and documentation for a
      command a commit adds was refused - it refused this release for
      naming tira.backup, which the other half of the same gate requires
      to be documented. It now resolves each dotted command to this
      working tree's own entrypoint, mirroring the dispatcher rather than
      approximating it.

1.32  2026-08-13
    - Only the process that launched a board may replace it. The version
      check runs in the closure that serves /data, which under a
      pre-forked server runs in a worker - and a worker is not the board:
      the master owns the listening socket, so a worker that re-executed
      could not bind the port, died, and took the request with it. The
      master forked a replacement, the next poll did the same thing, and
      the board never upgraded. Four boards had been doing that every
      sixty-five seconds for twenty hours, losing one request a minute.
      Reproduced on an isolated board before it was fixed: no skew and
      three polls is silent, a skew and ninety idle seconds is silent, a
      skew and one poll replaces a worker and returns no HTTP response at
      all, with "Cannot bind and listen to TCP port [Address already in
      use]" in the log from a process that had been a worker a moment
      earlier.
    - A served board therefore does not replace itself, and says so
      instead of failing quietly. When a different version is installed
      under it the page shows "Tira <version> is installed - restart this
      board to run it", beside where it already says when it last
      updated, and the board keeps working meanwhile. Proved in a real
      browser: the notice appears on a refresh, names the version, says
      what to do, and goes away when there is nothing to say.
    - This is also why the board's own refresh appeared to stop working.
      The poll was the request that killed the worker, so it returned
      nothing and the page kept what it had; clicking the browser's
      refresh button worked because that asks for the page rather than
      for /data.

1.31  2026-08-13
    - The bridge says a problem once, then leaves time to fix it before
      saying it again. Police passes every thirty seconds and every pass
      wrote every violation that was still true, so a problem nobody had
      got to yet said the same thing twice a minute indefinitely - the
      owner's own bridge carried one violation eighty-three times, still
      climbing, thirty seconds apart. A channel that repeats itself is one
      the reader learns to skim, and this is the channel that has to be
      read. A violation is now written at most once per quiet period, on a
      ladder of 5m, 15m, 30m and then an hour, so a problem that persists
      gets quieter rather than buzzing at one rate. What police reports as
      true is unchanged: a violation waiting out its quiet is still in the
      pass, marked quiet, because one that vanished while waiting would
      read as fixed. Nothing delays good news - a problem that has been
      fixed goes silent on the very next pass - and each violation has its
      own clock, so a new one is said at once whatever else is waiting.
    - "seen N times" counts the times it was said rather than the passes
      it survived, which is what the line has always claimed. "seen 5
      times, needs your attention" used to mean two and a half minutes of
      a thirty-second loop; it now means five tellings, and escalation to
      the owner's terminal follows the same count.
    - An agent that asks police for quiet spends no tellings while it
      lasts. It is not being told, so it is not being counted - otherwise
      it would come back from five minutes of silence owing the longest
      gap on the ladder for a problem nobody had mentioned to it.
    - A board restarts into new code, not into itself. The dashboard
      restarts when a new version is installed, and it decided that by
      comparing the label in .env against the version compiled into the
      module it had loaded - two things a restart cannot reconcile,
      because exec loads the same module again and disagrees again. When
      a label moved without its code, every check fired and every restart
      achieved nothing, for ever. It now asks whether restarting would
      run different code, by reading the version out of the module on
      disk, which is the file exec would load. Unknown on either side
      restarts nothing, as an unreadable .env already did.
    - The push gate runs the suite under a timeout. A test that leaked a
      web server left prove waiting on a pipe nothing would close - no
      output, no CPU and no end, which looks exactly like a slow run. A
      gate that can block for ever is one that gets skipped, so it fails
      instead.
    - A custom message on one of the six rules that read the machine has
      its parameters filled in. Two paths built a violation and only one
      substituted, so "the board has not been backed up in {age}" reached
      the bridge with the brace still in it - visible on the owner's own
      screen. Both paths now go through one expression, so they cannot
      drift apart again. Boards that never set a message saw nothing
      wrong, because the default wordings carry no parameters.

1.30  2026-08-13
    - The page saved to disk no longer offers a Columns button. It
      carried the button and the whole dialog with neither the script
      that opens them nor the binding that would, so it said "Columns"
      and did nothing when clicked - the second such control found on
      that page, after the queue toggles. Unlike those it could not
      simply be bound: editing columns posts to a server a saved page
      does not have, not temporarily but in principle, so the page does
      not offer it. The served board keeps it and it works there. Written
      as a rule rather than a fact about one button - every control the
      page offers is now asserted to be bound as well.

1.29  2026-08-13
    - The two queue toggles on the board page work. They render with
      titles and aria-pressed on the page a person looks at, and nothing
      was bound to them: applyQueue was defined and never connected,
      because the binding line sat inside the column-editor block, which
      the static page does not include. The other controls of that page -
      width, sort and filter - are all bound in the shared script, so
      these two were collateral damage of where one line was written. Two
      buttons that say what they do and do nothing when clicked. One line
      moved. Found by a browser test that had never been run.

1.28  2026-08-13
    - Naming the wrong card on a question command is refused rather than
      silently acting on another card. All six - answer, mark, update,
      discard, attach and voice - looked the question up by id and then
      overwrote the card the caller had named, so answering with one
      card's reference and another card's question changed the other card
      and reported success. The card that was named stayed waiting, and
      nothing anywhere said so: the board afterwards looked consistent,
      because the answer really was on a card. Resolving by id alone
      stays, since the ids are unique across a board; a card that is
      named and disagrees now gets a refusal saying where the question
      actually lives.

1.27  2026-08-13
    - The process table is read on macOS. ps prints the month before the
      day on Linux and after it on macOS, and both regexes that read the
      table required a month name in the second field - so on a Mac 192
      lines of ps output produced no processes at all and
      leftover-process reported nothing whatever was running. The month
      is now whichever of the two fields is a month name, which is
      decidable without knowing the platform and so cannot be wrong on a
      machine nobody anticipated. Proved on both labs: 177 of 178 on
      macOS where there had been none, and Linux unchanged.

1.26  2026-08-13
    - The world gatherer works on Windows. It asked whether a program
      existed with a search that looked for an exact filename and tested
      it with -x; on Windows a program is name.exe and -x answers for the
      extension, so on the lab it found neither git, nor ps, nor
      tasklist.exe sitting in System32. Every git fact and the process
      table came back empty, and five of the six rules that read the
      world were silent there whatever the machine was doing - the defect
      the previous releases were about, reintroduced inside its own fix.
      There is one search now rather than two that drift, the process
      table comes from tasklist on Windows and ps on POSIX with the
      platform passed in so both can be checked from either side, and
      PATHEXT is matched in either case for the filesystems that care.
      Proved on the Windows lab: 138 processes gathered where there were
      none.

1.25  2026-08-13
    - A rule can be considered and declined, with the reason, and police
      stops asking about it. The prompt lists every rule a project has
      not declared and prints on every run, which is right - but it could
      not tell "nobody has looked at this" from "somebody looked and said
      no", so on a board that had made those decisions it asked an
      answered question for ever. A channel that repeats itself is one
      everybody learns to read past, and this is the channel that exists
      to be read. The reason is required: without one this would be a way
      of silencing the prompt rather than a decision, and a decision with
      no reason recorded is indistinguishable from having skipped the
      question. Declaring a rule later clears its declining, and a rule
      that arrives in a future release is asked about as before.

1.24  2026-08-12
    - The two lines the owner reads now say who. The terminal escalation
      told him to "paste to the agent" as though there were one; it names
      the agent holding that card, or the core agent when nobody holds
      it. The onboarding prompt told him to keep the bridge running
      without saying who should be tailing it; it now says, and says
      something different for a chain. Both were found by reading a
      statement of work's acceptance criteria rather than its children -
      every child was done and "every place that assumed one agent is
      found and named" was still unmet, because only the obvious places
      had been handled. Naming the wrong recipient is worse than naming
      none, because he acts on it.

1.23  2026-08-12
    - One board is now worked both ways in the suite, and the policies
      guide names what differs. Five releases built the pieces and each
      proved its own in isolation; nothing had run the same board as a
      single agent and then as a chain and shown that every rule whose
      meaning changes behaves correctly in each. Three rules differ -
      wip-limit reads the project's number, card-sandbox-missing becomes
      real, and the bridge is read unfiltered at the top of a chain - and
      that list is now short, written down, and asserted. The rules that
      do not change are asserted not to, because "this rule is
      unaffected" is a claim too.

1.22  2026-08-12
    - A card outlives the agent working it. A card agent closes when its
      turn ends, and waking it has to be a resume rather than a fresh
      agent - a fresh one works everything out again, which costs tokens
      and produces answers inconsistent with what the card already says.
      So the handle to resume lives on the card, read off the board by
      the parent that may wake it, because the thing that spawned the
      agent is the thing that closes. A child with no agent yet is listed
      with nothing to resume, so the answer is every child rather than
      only the started ones.
    - And what passed between the user and whoever was working a card is
      recorded on it. In a chain the user talks only to the core agent,
      so without this a manager knows what it said downward and nothing
      of what came back - it is managing something it cannot see. Kept
      separate from comments, which are somebody writing on the card
      rather than a record of something said elsewhere.
    - Tira spawns nothing and resumes nothing. It records what the thing
      that does needs to find.

1.21  2026-08-12
    - How much may be in flight at once is the project's answer rather
      than a number Tira picks. A work-in-progress limit counts the whole
      board, and no one number is right for both a single agent and a
      chain of six - two is sensible for one and absurd for the other. So
      tira.project.limit stores what the owner says, a policy declares its
      column and leaves the number alone, and the rule reads it when it
      runs: raising it quiets the rule without touching any policy. A
      policy may still carry its own --max, which is narrower and wins,
      and a policy with neither is refused when it is declared.

1.20  2026-08-12
    - A card records the work tree it is being worked in, and
      card-sandbox-missing checks the record rather than a name. A work
      tree existing on the machine says nothing about which card it
      belongs to: matched by name alone, one left behind by a card
      finished last week satisfies the rule for a card started this
      morning. So the agent that makes the tree records it on the card,
      and the rule says three different things because each wants a
      different fix - no tree at all, a well-named tree nobody has
      claimed, or a card claiming a tree that is not there. Tira still
      makes no work trees.

1.19  2026-08-12
    - Every bridge line about a card says the way down to it, so the one
      reader in a chain can route it. In a chain of agents the core agent
      is the only one reading the bridge and hands a message to the
      card's manager rather than to the card's agent, and working out who
      that is by going back to the board is what the bridge exists to
      avoid - a card reparented since would rewrite what was already
      said. A card with nothing above it says so; a violation about no
      card at all carries no path. What a single agent sees is unchanged.

1.18  2026-08-12
    - A project can say whether one agent works it or a chain of them
      does, and tira.onboard asks before it creates anything. Multi-agent
      is built on single agent rather than beside it: one agent per card,
      each named for the card and managed by the agent that owns its
      parent. Several rules mean different things between the two and
      nothing recorded which, so reading it off the board would have been
      wrong the first day one agent assigned two cards to two names. A
      project that has never been asked answers with nothing and behaves
      exactly as it always has, which is every board that already exists.

1.17  2026-08-12
    - leftover-container no longer reports every container on the
      machine. It took an age and nothing else, so the first time police
      could see containers at all it named eleven, of which nine
      belonged to other projects entirely - a trading terminal, two web
      sites, an Obsidian server. Naming somebody else's running work is
      an invitation to go and stop it, and a rule reporting what nobody
      here can act on is one everybody learns to read past. It now takes
      a pattern, as leftover-process always has, and a policy declared
      without one is refused rather than quietly matching everything.
      An existing declaration with no pattern will need one.

1.16  2026-08-12
    - Police looks at the machine it was always meant to look at. Six
      rules read the world rather than the board - leftover processes
      and containers, commits naming no card, a tree changing with
      nothing on the board, unpushed work, and how long since the last
      backup - and the command handed the engine five empty lists and
      nothing else. Every one of them evaluated against nothing and
      reported nothing, on every run, while passing every test it had,
      because the tests handed the engine a world of their own. Found
      when board-unbacked said the board had never been backed up while
      the backups the push gate writes on every release sat in place.
      The facts are now gathered where a shell is allowed, so Tira
      itself still invokes none, and each rule is proved by making the
      condition real: a repository with commits nobody pushed, a process
      actually running, a tree actually dirty. Running it against this
      repository then found the same silence one level in: git was
      asked for a branch's upstream, which errors on a repository that
      has origin/master and no upstream configured - so unpushed work
      and commits naming no card went unreported on the board that most
      needed them, and git said so on somebody else's terminal. It now
      asks quietly and falls back to the branch it was actually pushed
      to.
    - The policy bridge carries words that are not plain ASCII. It was
      written raw, so a card titled in Chinese warned "Wide character in
      print" on the owner's own terminal and wrote bytes that an agent's
      name could not be matched against - a line lost while everything
      reported success. Written and read as UTF-8 now, like every other
      file here.
    - The push gate no longer litters the board it checks. Its probe
      card - deliberately hollow, raised to prove the gate refuses it -
      was discarded in silence, so twenty of them accumulated looking
      exactly like work somebody had given up on. It says why before it
      sets one aside, and card-holes refuses a discard with no reason,
      so the gate catches this the way it catches every other hole.

1.15  2026-08-12
    - The board keeps answering while one connection is slow. It was
      served by a listener that handles one connection at a time, so a
      single request that never finished stopped it answering anybody -
      the port open, the process alive, and nothing coming back, which
      looks exactly like a board that is fine until somebody tries to
      load it. Reproduced deliberately, then fixed by serving with
      Starman whether or not there is a certificate: one server rather
      than two that behave differently under load, since `--ssl`
      already avoided this and plain HTTP did not.

1.14  2026-08-12
    - A work-in-progress limit says who is holding what. It already
      counted the board rather than the agent, which is the useful
      meaning when one agent carries one ticket - but the message read
      exactly the same whether three agents had one card each or one
      agent had three, and those are opposite situations. It now names
      the holder of every card, and says `nobody` for a card nobody is
      carrying rather than letting it look owned.

1.13  2026-08-12
    - Quiet belongs to the agent that asked for it. Suspension was one
      switch for the whole board, so an agent asking for five minutes
      to concentrate stopped every other agent hearing about its own
      work - and stopped the owner seeing anything at all, which was
      the sharper half of it. The quiet is applied at the bridge rather
      than at the sweep, so police still finds everything and still
      reports to the owner's terminal; only what is written to that one
      agent changes. The ceiling, the reason, the renewal marking and
      the enforcement log are exactly as they were, and a suspension
      naming nobody is still board-wide.

1.12  2026-08-12
    - The bridge speaks to whoever is carrying the card. One agent per
      ticket is the model, so an agent's concern is exactly one card,
      and a bridge that hands every agent every violation is noise by
      construction - and a channel that becomes noise is one everybody
      learns to read past. An agent narrows it by saying who it is,
      with `--author` or `TIRA_AUTHOR` in the environment. A card
      assigned to nobody still reaches everybody, because filtering
      that loses it trades noise for silence; so does anything that is
      not about a card at all; and reading without naming an agent
      still shows the whole board, which is how the owner watches it.

1.11  2026-08-12
    - The board can be served over HTTPS. `tira.dashboard -o browser
      --ssl` uses a certificate the board makes for itself the first
      time and reuses afterwards - a certificate that changed on every
      restart would make a browser warn every time, which teaches
      somebody to click through warnings. It is made by a library
      rather than by running `openssl`, because Tira invokes no shell
      or external process and that guarantee is not worth spending on
      a convenience; it lives beside the project rather than inside a
      board, with the key readable by nobody else. The board says what
      it is worth on the terminal it starts from: it stops somebody
      reading your password off the wire, and does not stop somebody
      who can already stand between you and the machine. That matters
      more now that a session can be told never to expire.

1.10  2026-08-12
    - An attachment can be taken off a card without losing it.
      `tira.attachment.discard` sets one aside the way everything else
      in Tira is set aside: the reference stays on the card stamped
      with when and by whom, the browser draws it struck through and
      greyed, and the work log reads the event off the card rather
      than being told - so it cannot be forgotten and cannot be
      written by hand. The stored file is untouched even when that was
      the last reference to it, because the bytes are shared by
      content hash and are not one card's to destroy. The cross on a
      chip in the browser now discards rather than deletes; deleting
      is still possible from the command line, where somebody has said
      they mean it.

    - A board can be told to stop expiring its sessions.
      `tira.dashboard -o browser --no-session-expire` serves one whose
      sign-in lasts until somebody signs out. The default is unchanged
      and deliberate - ten idle minutes ends a session, and the board's
      own refresh does not count as activity, so a tab left open
      overnight does not keep itself signed in. What that costs a board
      somebody watches all day was never measured until now: it expires
      exactly as fast as a board nobody is looking at, and every
      refresh after that is refused. The board says on the terminal it
      starts from that sessions never expire, and what that means over
      plain HTTP.

1.07  2026-08-12

    - TKT-026, TKT-055, TKT-056, TKT-058. The documentation gate only
      read commands written as literal command lines, so a heading
      promising a command that did not exist, or a whole argument
      table of invented flags, sailed through - two flags had been
      shipping documented and unusable, `--with-questions` on the
      dashboard and `--sandbox` on tira.policy.add. Widened to read
      headings and argument tables too, and both flags now exist,
      because the documentation described what was intended.

    - `parent-ahead-of-children` reports a parent whose child is not
      finished at every level - a statement of work above its epics,
      an epic above its tickets, a ticket above its sub-tickets -
      where each check previously looked at a card on its own and saw
      nothing. Which column means finished comes from the board's own
      roles; a board that has never declared one is reported
      unresolved rather than guessed at, and the push gate refuses it
      too.

    - The work log lost every entry except comments on attribution -
      "who did it" read nobody - and a background refresh wiped the
      questions and work-log sections entirely, since redrawing the
      card rebuilt the sections they live in without putting them
      back. A refresh now redraws all three; an open log keeps its
      state and follows the card; an unattributed row keeps its
      columns; and TIRA_AUTHOR lets a command-line caller say once who
      they are.

    - Police now hands the owner a prompt to give the agent on every
      run: for a project with nothing declared, or naming the rules a
      project set up before them is not using. Silent for a project
      already using everything, because nagging is how a prompt stops
      being read.

    - A card past verify with no gate and no evidence recorded is now
      a hole the push gate refuses.

1.08  2026-08-12
    - No pure-Perl parsers. `Cpanel::JSON::XS` and `YAML::XS` are
      required rather than preferred, and a missing one is refused with
      the command to install it instead of falling back to something
      slow. The measurement behind the owner's rule: decoding a mature
      board of 138 records averaging 32KB cost 1992ms with the
      pure-Perl parser and 6ms with the compiled one, while reading the
      same files without parsing costs 2ms - the parser was the board
      walk. Nothing on disk moves: a record written, read back and
      written again is byte-identical, and booleans stay
      `JSON::PP::Boolean`, which is the class both compiled parsers
      produce. Proved on macOS and Windows, because requiring a
      compiled module makes a compiler an install requirement.

    - A project whose text is not ASCII can be read again. libyaml does
      its own UTF-8 - it hands back encoded bytes and expects them -
      and Tira was decoding before handing them over, so the second
      byte of a two-byte character was read as a leading octet and the
      file was refused. A project with a pound sign in a person's name
      was unreadable, which reads as a corrupt board rather than a
      parser fault.

    - The journal writes bytes. It encoded characters and wrote them to
      a raw handle, so any card with non-ASCII text put a warning on
      stderr and broken bytes into the journal - which is what the work
      log reads. Silent under the old parser and loud under the new
      one, and wrong under both.

    - The work log says who. Every entry except a comment recorded
      what happened and not who did it, so the log could not answer
      the question it exists to answer: whether the agent did the work
      or somebody else did. The browser always knew, because there is
      a login in front of it; the command line now takes TIRA_AUTHOR
      from the environment, said once rather than remembered on every
      command, and `--author` still beats it. With nobody named
      anywhere an entry says nobody rather than inventing a name.

    - A work log left open follows the card. It fetched once when
      somebody expanded it and never again - but underneath that, the
      background refresh redrew the card alone, and redrawing the card
      rebuilds the sections the questions and the work log live in. So
      both sections were wiped on every refresh and never put back, and
      an owner watching a card saw nothing happen while it was
      happening. A refresh now redraws all three, the log keeps its
      open state, and a log nobody has opened still fetches nothing.

    - An entry with nobody named keeps its shape. The name cell was
      left out rather than left blank, so the detail slid into the
      name column and the row read as though it were broken.

    - Police notices a parent that says it is finished while its
      children are not - a statement of work above its epics, an epic
      above its tickets, or a ticket above its sub-tickets. Every other
      check looks at a card on its own, so nothing saw it. Which column
      means finished comes from the board's roles; a board that has
      never said is reported as unresolved rather than guessed at. The
      push gate refuses it too.

    - Two flags the documentation promised and the command line never
      accepted: `--with-questions` on the dashboard and `--sandbox` on
      `tira.policy.add`. Both were found by teaching the documentation
      check to read headings and argument tables, which is where almost
      every flag in this project is written down and none of them were
      being checked.

1.06  2026-08-12
    - A search index that cannot disagree with the files.
      `tira.search.index` builds one, and searching then skips a card
      whose text cannot match without parsing it - parsing being what
      reading a board actually costs. Each row is keyed by the content
      hash of the file it describes, so a row can only ever describe
      the exact bytes on disk: edit a card behind Tira's back and
      search follows the file. There is no stale state to detect and
      nothing to fall back from. A corrupt, missing or restored-over
      index is not an error - search reads the files, as it did before
      any index existed - and a project that never runs the command
      has no index and needs no SQLite at all.

    - Tira works on Windows. It never did: every write is a write to
      a temporary file beside the target followed by a rename over
      it, and `rename` on Windows refuses when the destination
      exists, so Tira could create a file and never change one. Every
      card update, board change and config write failed. The
      replacement is now `MoveFileEx` with `MOVEFILE_REPLACE_EXISTING`
      there, which is a same-volume atomic replace rather than a
      delete followed by a rename, so what the documentation promises
      stays true. Behind that sat a second cause with the same
      symptom: `YAML::PP`'s `load_file` leaves the file open, and an
      open handle on Windows makes a file impossible to replace, so
      every board config write failed immediately after the config
      had been read. Tira now reads YAML through its own reader and
      closes the file when it says so. Neither fault was visible on
      Linux and both were total on Windows; the suite was green
      throughout.

    - Timestamps, program lookup and output bytes no longer assume a
      POSIX machine. The default clock computes its offset rather than
      asking `strftime`, whose `%z` is a numeric offset on Linux and a
      zone name on Windows, so a record written there carried a stamp
      nothing could parse. The search for an installed coding agent
      uses the platform's own path separator and executable
      extensions instead of a colon and a bare name, so on Windows it
      finds what is installed rather than reporting nothing. And the
      command line removes the text-mode layer from its output, so
      the bytes that leave the process are the bytes Tira produced -
      which the read cache depends on, because it stores those bytes
      and serves them back.

    - The read cache can no longer serve a caller its own stale data.
      It decided whether it was current from the modification times of
      the board's directories, and a card edited in place adds and
      removes no name - so on Windows the directory was untouched, the
      cache was judged current, and the change the caller had just
      made was invisible to it. The fingerprint now reads the records
      themselves.

    - The check that runs every documented example no longer leaves a
      scratch board behind. It runs from the push gate, so one left
      behind was not one directory but one per release; twelve of them
      accumulated in a single night. The gate's own prover now counts
      them and refuses if any survive.

    - Reminders reach a coding agent on Windows. The reminder script
      carried its own copy of the search for an installed agent -
      splitting the path on a colon and looking for a name with no
      extension - so it decided nothing was installed however much
      was, exited quietly, and a user would have set reminders up and
      never heard anything. It asks the same subroutine the command
      line asks now, and the delivery is proved on the lab with an
      agent standing up the way Windows has one.

    - A dashboard on Windows never picked up a new version. The
      entrypoint to restart into was found by asking whether a file is
      executable, and there is no such bit there, so nothing was ever
      found however many versions were installed.

    - A board that loses its session says so. Its background refresh
      came back refused and the page, having nothing to do with a
      failure, kept drawing the cards it last managed to load - so a
      board could sit for a day showing work that had long since
      moved. A refused refresh now puts the sign-in in front of the
      person and takes away the board it can no longer vouch for.

    - A project directory is resolved to its real path, so every way
      of spelling one directory is one project and the same project
      cannot register two collectors racing the same board. The
      behaviour was always there and had never been asserted; the
      macOS lab found it, where /var is a symlink to /private/var.

1.05  2026-08-11
    - A login in front of the browser dashboard. A person claims a
      password the first time they use the board and must match it
      afterwards; only a salted, iterated digest is ever stored, and
      never the password. A person whose id or name says "bot" cannot
      sign in at all, checked before the stored hash is consulted so a
      hash written by hand is not a way around it. Sessions live on
      disk and survive the dashboard restarting itself, expiring after
      ten minutes of no activity - the board's own background poll
      reads a session without extending it, so a tab left open
      overnight does not keep itself signed in. Every route is behind
      the gate: the front door serves the login page and everything
      else refuses with JSON, because the board fetches its own routes
      and a login page rendered into a card is worse than an error.
      Over plain HTTP the password and cookie travel in clear, which
      the documentation says plainly rather than leaving to be found.

    - A self-governing subsystem. The agent declares what a project
      cares about with `tira.policy`; the owner runs `tira.police` in a
      terminal of their own and it reports what it finds; the agent
      runs `tira.policy.bridge` and acts on it. Police never writes to
      the board - it watches read-only and writes only to a log it
      owns, because two writers in one board is how this project lost
      its own board on the day the subsystem was designed. Twenty
      rules, from a card that left the backlog as a title to a test
      container nobody stopped. Every violation carries a number,
      keeps it, and climbs four tones as it persists; past five
      repeats it reaches the owner's terminal with a message he can
      paste straight to the agent. Fixing the cause silences it on the
      next pass, with nothing to acknowledge.

    - Policies can be declared on the project, a board, a column or a
      single card, and the most specific one wins - per rule, so a card
      that overrides one keeps every other rule the project set.

    - Columns can be told what they mean. A rule written against a role
      follows the meaning rather than the name, so it still applies
      after somebody renames the column. The vocabulary belongs to the
      project; Tira matches a role without needing to understand it.

    - `docs/POLICIES.md`, printed by `tira.policies`, with an
      onboarding walk-through and a hundred worked use cases. Every
      command in it is run by the test suite rather than read, because
      a document full of examples that do not work teaches an agent
      that the surface is unreliable.

1.04  2026-08-10
    - The Discard column was offering an add-card control. Showing the
      column in `1.01` brought its controls with it, and inviting
      somebody to create work directly into the discard pile is not an
      invitation worth making. The column is still shown; only the
      control is gone.

1.03  2026-08-10
    - A question that had been set aside was still showing somewhere to
      drop a file. The drop zone is an invitation, and on a question
      nobody will act on again it invites something that cannot happen
      - and a control that does nothing teaches people to distrust the
      ones that do.
    - Files already on a discarded question still show, because they
      still happened and somebody reading the history may want them. A
      discarded question with none now shows nothing but the question,
      struck through, and when it was set aside.

1.02  2026-08-10
    - The self-restart added in `0.93` killed the very dashboard it was
      meant to keep current. It re-executed `$0`, which is the
      dispatcher rather than the Tira entrypoint when the board is
      launched through one, so upgrading under a running board re-ran
      the wrong program and the process exited.
    - The entrypoint is now derived from the command being run, checked
      before anything is replaced, and the project is passed explicitly
      so the new process does not have to rediscover it from a working
      directory or an environment variable. If no valid entrypoint can
      be found the board simply carries on: a restart that cannot work
      is worse than a stale board.
    - My test had called the code directly, where `$0` is the test
      file, so the assumption held and it passed. It proved the
      mechanism under conditions the mechanism never meets. The
      reproduction now sets `$0` to a dispatcher path deliberately.

1.01  2026-08-10
    - The Discard column now appears on a board somebody is looking at.
      Every board is created with a Backlog and a Discard; the owner
      saw the first and never the second, so discarding a card made it
      vanish from the only view he uses. `--include-discard` would have
      shown it, but nobody passes a flag for something they do not know
      is missing.
    - Drawn faded and marked as set aside, so it reads as an archive
      rather than as more work outstanding. The ref-only listing an
      agent queries still leaves it out, because that path exists to be
      cheap, and the machine formats are unchanged unless asked.

1.00  2026-08-10
    - Evidence belongs with the question it explains. An agent hangs
      files on a question with `tira.question.attach`, the owner
      attaches while answering with `tira.question.answer --file`, and
      both are listed under that question, because somebody reading it
      wants everything bearing on it rather than a tidy taxonomy.
    - `tira.attachment.list --question Q-007` narrows to one question;
      naming none still lists every file on the card, each saying where
      it hangs. Fetching needs only the reference - no card, no
      question, nothing to choose.
    - On the board, a file can be dropped straight onto a question, and
      onto its answer once there is one. The answer box grows with what
      is typed instead of hiding the start of it.
    - A file dropped on the board kept the name of the temporary file
      it arrived in rather than the name it was dropped under. Caught
      by a test written against the real provider rather than a mock.
    - Both manuals now have to name every question command, not just
      the reference. A command missing from the manual is one most
      agents will never find, however well the reference describes it.

0.99  2026-08-10
    - A question that has been marked collapses to its question, its
      answer and a tick or a cross. Everything else - the reason, the
      choices, the play control, the answer box, the mark buttons - is
      only in the way once there is nothing left to do. On a real card
      that is 113 pixels against 398.
    - The panel is ordered by what still needs doing: unanswered,
      answered but unjudged, judged, set aside. Sorted on a copy, so
      displaying a card never reorders the card itself.
