0.80  2026-08-09
    - Questions on cards (EPIC-469, first part). An agent that cannot
      move a card can still ask about it, and the answer lives on the
      card rather than in whatever file that agent happened to keep.
      `tira.question.ask`, `.list`, `.answer`, `.update`, `.mark` and
      `.discard`.
    - References are project-wide with a `Q` prefix - one sequence
      across all three boards - so `Q-007` reaches a question without
      anybody naming the card it was asked on. Cards are addressed by
      their own reference alone for the same reason: the reference
      already names the board.
    - Status is derived rather than stored, so it cannot drift: no
      answer is `new`, an answer is `answered`, set aside is
      `discarded`. Nothing is ever really deleted - a discarded
      question keeps its answer underneath it.
    - Listing the answers is what marks them read, and writes only when
      something actually changed. The mark on an answer is separate
      from having read it, and a cross settles nothing on its own.
      Every list carries the instruction for what to do next.
    - Documented in `docs/commands.md` and as use cases UC-101 to
      UC-103, so an agent meeting Tira through either manual finds it.

0.79  2026-08-09
    - `tira.usage` prints the command reference, the way `tira.skills`
      prints the agent manual. The reference lists every command and
      argument, but until now it could only be read by somebody who
      already knew the file existed and where to look, which is no use
      to an agent working through the dashboard.
    - Each manual now names the other and the command that prints it,
      so whichever one is reached first leads to the second instead of
      being a dead end.

0.78  2026-08-09
    - The column editor's grip, eye and remove icons reached the
      browser as mojibake. The renderer has no `use utf8`, so a literal
      glyph in the source is read as separate bytes and then encoded
      again on the way out; the rest of the script already used escapes
      for exactly this reason and these four did not. Written as
      escapes now, and every embedded script and stylesheet is asserted
      to be pure ASCII in both the static and the live rendering, so a
      literal glyph anywhere in the renderer fails the suite.

0.77  2026-08-08
    - Onboarding collected the reminder settings and then did nothing
      with them: the collector name went into the project file and
      registering the job was a separate command that nothing called,
      so filling it in produced no collector anywhere while looking
      like it had worked. Onboarding now registers it, and reports the
      name it will really answer to - `tira.<name>`, not the bare name
      that was typed - together with the command that starts it.
    - The interval is asked once instead of twice. The staleness
      threshold is the meaningful number, and the heartbeat follows it,
      because there is no point looking more often than the shortest
      window that could make anything stale. An explicit `--heartbeat`
      still wins.
    - The directory question offers whatever project is already
      resolvable, so re-running onboarding from anywhere pre-fills
      instead of asking for the path first.

0.76  2026-08-08
    - Fit all now wraps the columns onto more than one row. With the
      overflow defects fixed, thirteen columns across a 1280px window
      was 81 pixels each: correct arithmetic and an unreadable board.
      A table cannot wrap, because the heading row and the body row are
      separate and a wrapped column would leave its heading behind - so
      each column is now one block owning its own heading, laid out in
      a scrolling row for Standard and a wrapping grid for Fit.
    - Almost nothing else needed changing: the card drag finds the list
      under the pointer by geometry, and paging, filtering and counts
      address columns by name. Verified with the drag, paging, wrap and
      column-editor browser guards, each run twice.

0.75  2026-08-08
    - Creating a project inside another one is refused, naming the
      project in the way and its directory. Without a directory,
      creation happens where the person is standing, and project
      discovery walks upward - so a project made inside another is
      buried, cards go somewhere nobody thinks to look, and afterwards
      either project may answer depending on where a command runs, with
      no error at any point. `--nested` still allows it on purpose,
      because the hazard is doing it by accident rather than doing it
      at all. A directory that is already a project is untouched: that
      is adoption and it has its own rules.

0.74  2026-08-08
    - Mutations that bypassed the project lock now take it. Comments,
      checklists, gates, evidence and attachment references read a
      record, changed it in memory and wrote it back with nothing held,
      so two changes made at the same moment could each read the same
      record and one could be lost without any error - while the manual
      already claimed every mutation was serialised.
    - The lock is now reentrant. Two handles on one file are two lock
      entries even inside one process, so a locked operation taking the
      lock again used to deadlock against itself; that is why those
      methods were left outside it. A root already held now runs inside
      the lock we have, and each of the ten methods wraps its whole
      read-modify-write rather than only the write, because the read is
      half the race.

0.73  2026-08-08
    - The column editor. Each board control now has a Columns button
      opening a modal showing that board's own structure: drag a row by
      its grip to reorder, edit the label, set how long a card may sit
      there, turn the eye off to stop the column being chased, remove a
      column, or add one. Saving sends the whole layout in a single
      call.
    - Reordering uses pointer events rather than HTML5 drag, matching
      the card drag the board already uses, so the grip works on a
      phone rather than being the one thing on the board a touch device
      cannot move. Protected columns offer no remove control at all,
      since the engine refuses them and a button that always errors is
      worse than no button. An untouched threshold is omitted rather
      than sent as zero, so a column falls back to the project default
      instead of being refused.

0.72  2026-08-08
    - `tira.column.apply` takes a whole column layout in one call and
      works out the difference itself: what to add, what to remove,
      what order to sit in, and which labels, thresholds and watched
      flags to carry. An editor knows the layout it wants, not the
      sequence of steps that reaches it, so that work belongs here
      rather than in the browser. Available to the dashboard through
      its own provider and route.
    - Reordering, labels, thresholds and watched flags apply as a
      single write; removals move cards and each takes the project
      lock, which is not reentrant, so they run first and one at a
      time. The result says what was done, because a run that fails
      partway through several removals will already have made some.

0.71  2026-08-08
    - The reminder job itself (EPIC-457, last piece).
      `tira.collector.show` computes the background job for a project
      and `tira.collector.install` registers it, merging into the
      machine's own configuration rather than editing an installed
      skill - which the runtime destroys on the next reinstall - and
      refusing to take over a name another project owns. No heartbeat
      means no job.
    - Sending lives in `collector/tira-remind`, outside `lib/` and
      outside the command surface, so Tira itself still runs no shell
      and no external process. It sends one message covering every
      stale card, records them only once delivery has actually
      happened, retries a failed delivery once, and then writes a
      warning. Nothing stale, no coding agent installed and no session
      configured are quiet no-ops rather than errors, so a situation
      that is not a fault does not light an error indicator or feed the
      runtime watchdog.

0.70  2026-08-08
    - Reminder settings and re-runnable onboarding (EPIC-457, sixth
      piece). The project records the default staleness threshold, the
      coding agent, its session, the heartbeat and the name of the job;
      all of them settable on `tira.project.update` as well as through
      `tira.onboard`. Onboarding now asks the directory first, which is
      what lets it be run again on an existing project with every
      answer pre-filled - and naming a different directory reloads that
      project's own settings instead of carrying the first one's over.
      With no coding agent installed, the questions about one never
      appear. An empty value clears a setting; no heartbeat means no
      reminders.
    - The reminder ladder now has ten levels rather than five, and the
      upper ones are far more forceful. The audience is a coding agent
      rather than a person, so the top of the ladder is absolute about
      priority in a way a message to a human would not be.

0.69  2026-08-08
    - The escalating reminder itself (EPIC-457, fifth piece). One
      message covers every card past its column's limit, and its tone
      rises with how often those cards have already been chased where
      they stand: plain, tense, angry, shouting, then a final tone that
      keeps counting instead of running out of words. The most-chased
      card sets the tone, so a long-stuck card is never softened by
      newer company, while each line states its own count, its column
      and how long it has sat there. Nothing stale composes nothing at
      all. `tira.notify.compose` returns the whole thing in one call.
      The wording is proposed, not final - nothing sends until the
      owner has approved it.

0.68  2026-08-08
    - Unattended failures now have somebody to tell (EPIC-457, fourth
      piece). A failure that background work cannot resolve is recorded
      once and shown under the output of whatever command anybody runs
      next, naming the command that clears it, and it keeps appearing
      until somebody does. The same failure recurring leaves the
      standing warning alone instead of piling up copies.
      `tira.warning.list`, `tira.warning.add` and `tira.warning.clear`
      manage them. Human output carries the notice on standard output;
      machine formats carry it on standard error, so a JSON or TOON
      payload stays parseable for the agent reading it.

0.67  2026-08-08
    - Notification history in SQLite beside the project file
      (EPIC-457, third piece). One row per delivered reminder holding
      the card, its column and the time; the escalation level is how
      many rows that card already has in the column it is sitting in,
      so a move resets escalation for free and the card is never
      rewritten. `tira.notify.record` and `tira.notify.list` expose it,
      and `tira.stale --stale --with-level` reports each stale card
      with the level it has reached. Reading creates no database, so a
      project that has never notified needs SQLite for nothing; when it
      is needed and missing, the message says to install SQLite rather
      than showing a Perl module failing to load.

0.66  2026-08-08
    - Per-column staleness limits and watching (EPIC-457, second
      piece). Each column carries its own limit in minutes and its own
      watched flag on the board config, set with the new
      `tira.column.update`; `tira.project.update --notify-after` sets
      the project-wide default a column falls back to.
      `tira.stale --stale` then judges each card by its own column's
      limit and skips unwatched columns entirely, so a hundred-card
      backlog or a finished column can be kept out of the report. A
      column is watched unless switched off, so boards created before
      this release need no migration.

0.65  2026-08-08
    - New `tira.stale` (EPIC-457, the first piece): how long every card
      has sat in the column it is in now, measured from its most recent
      recorded column move, across all three boards in one call.
      `--older-than MINUTES` filters to the ones past a limit. A card
      whose column entry was never recorded is reported with no
      duration rather than a guessed one and never counts as old -
      history only began in 0.53, and inventing an entry time would put
      most of an existing board into the first report. Unreadable
      stamps degrade to unknown instead of failing the board, and a
      column renamed underneath a card leaves its measurement intact.

0.64  2026-08-08
    - Columns now show ten cards with a button to reveal ten more at a
      time, saying how many remain (owner request). The count beside the
      column name stays the column's real total rather than the visible
      slice.
    - The board control gained a keyword filter. It asks the server, so
      a word that appears only in a card's description still finds it;
      the box is debounced, an empty box clears the filter, a failed
      request narrows to nothing rather than quietly showing
      everything, and filtering restarts paging at the first ten
      matches.

0.63  2026-08-08
    - Bulk card moves (owner request): shift-click selects and
      deselects, dragging any selected card carries the whole selection
      to the target column with the ghost showing the batch size, and a
      plain click clears the selection and opens that card. The
      selection is kept by reference so it survives the live refresh
      that rebuilds every card. Each card posts the same validated move
      as a single drag - no bulk endpoint and no new server semantics.
    - Fit mode now runs the board edge to edge, recovering the side
      gutters: on a thirteen-column board the columns go from 81px to
      86px.

0.62  2026-08-08
    - The browser tab now reads `<project> :: <board> :: <cards>`, for
      example `MT5 :: Tickets :: 57`, instead of a generic product name
      on every board (owner request). All three boards on one page read
      `Kanban`, and the count is what the page actually renders, so it
      never disagrees with the board beneath it.

0.61  2026-08-08
    - Fixed cards still drawing over the next column on boards with many
      columns (owner photo, message 3007) — a different defect from
      0.60's, in a different mode. The card list is a CSS grid, and a
      grid item refuses to shrink below its own content by default, so
      once a column is narrower than a card's longest word the card
      overflowed. 0.60 fixed the columns; this fixes the cards. The
      wide-board guard now also runs a thirteen-column board.

0.60  2026-08-08
    - Fixed board columns collapsing and cards spilling into the next
      column on boards with many columns (owner photo, message 3005).
      The table uses a fixed layout, which takes every column's width
      from the header row alone: the headers carried only a minimum
      width, so nine columns were divided into 134px each and the cards
      overflowed. The headers now carry the width itself, so Standard
      keeps its 17rem columns and scrolls as it always claimed to. Fit
      mode is unchanged. New nine-column browser guard, because the
      two-column fixture could not express the defect.

0.59  2026-08-08
    - A leading ~ now means the home directory wherever a path is
      accepted (owner report: answering the directory question with
      ~/foo/bar created a directory literally named ~). That covers the
      guided prompts and a quoted --dir or --project, which a shell
      never expands.
    - Prompts are editable at a terminal: Ctrl-A and Ctrl-E as asked,
      plus Ctrl-U, Ctrl-K, backspace, arrows and Home/End, written
      against core POSIX termios rather than a Term::ReadLine editing
      module, none of which is installed anywhere this runs. Away from a
      terminal the prompt stays a plain read.
    - project.update gained --listen HOST[:PORT] as the compact form of
      --dashboard-host and --dashboard-port.
    - Fixed: pressing enter past the people question in the guided setup
      reported "Every member needs a name" instead of meaning "none".

0.58  2026-08-07
    - A project now remembers its dashboard address:
      `tira.project.update --dashboard-host localhost --dashboard-port
      8080` stores it, project.show reports it, and `-o browser` uses it
      instead of always listening on 0.0.0.0:7899. `any` is the
      plain-language form of every interface. An address given on the
      command line still wins, and both values are validated where they
      are set rather than where they are used.

0.57  2026-08-07
    - New `tira.onboard`: the guided form of project.new, asking for the
      name, directory, people, each board's reference prefix, whether the
      boards share one column set, and the columns, then confirming
      before it writes anything. Flags pre-fill the answers, unusable
      answers are re-asked with the reason, declining exits 1 and
      reaching the end of input aborts — both leaving nothing behind.
      Only onboard ever prompts; project.new stays argument-driven so
      scripts and agents can never be left waiting on input.
    - project.new gained --sow-columns, --epic-columns, and
      --ticket-columns, so boards can differ instead of always sharing
      one set.

0.56  2026-08-07
    - New `tira.project.new` (owner request): one command creates a
      project, its members, each board's reference prefix, and one
      shared column set across all three boards — replacing a
      thirty-five command manual sequence. Column names are given as
      they read ("Done / Release") and slugified automatically with the
      original text kept as the label; columns that already exist,
      including the protected Backlog and Discard, are skipped, so the
      natural list works verbatim and re-running changes nothing. All
      input is validated before the first write, and prefixes are set
      before any record can exist so the first reference is 001.

0.55  2026-08-07
    - Fixed the card dialog's column dropdown showing the card count
      appended to each column name (owner photo, message 2990) — a
      0.54 regression: the option labels were read from the whole
      header cell, which now contains the count badge. Labels now come
      from the column-name element. Card moves were never affected;
      only the visible text was.

0.54  2026-08-07
    - Column card counts and add-card from the board (owner message
      2978). Each column header shows its count, hidden when the column
      is empty, derived from the board so it stays right after refresh,
      drag, and creation. Every column on a live board gains an add-card
      control that opens the dialog in new-card mode: empty form, no
      reference until it is saved, title the only required field, the
      rest optional and editable on the card afterwards. Creation goes
      through the same validated engine path as the CLI and the dialog
      then switches to the created card.

0.53  2026-08-07
    - Per-field history (DD-443): every record write is journaled field
      by field to .tira/history/<REF>.jsonl, whichever command performed
      it, with before and after values, the change time, the operation,
      and an optional validated author. Creation seeds birth entries,
      moves journal their column change, structural fields record that
      they changed without inlining their value, and rolled-back
      operations record nothing. tira.history.list reads it with the
      established window, since, where, count, and truncation
      semantics. History lives outside the boards, so records, content
      hashes, and board reads are untouched.

0.52  2026-08-07
    - Reads are up to 170x faster on mature boards: Cpanel::JSON::XS is
      used when installed, with core JSON::PP as the fallback. Measured
      on 138 records averaging 32KB — dashboard with titles 2076ms to
      12ms, full export 2070ms to 19ms, field-scoped search 2234ms to
      17ms. Output is byte-identical, so stored records never rewrite
      and content hashes never drift; the non-reference tolerance of
      the previous backend is preserved explicitly on every
      value-bearing path.

0.51  2026-08-07
    - Column-width toggle in every board header (owner photo, message
      2976): Standard keeps today's fixed-width scrollable columns, Fit
      all shrinks every column so a wide board — the reported case had
      twelve — is fully visible without sideways scrolling. The choice
      applies to all boards, persists in browser storage, degrades to
      Standard when storage is blocked, and narrow screens keep
      scrollable columns while the desktop preference is preserved.

0.50  2026-08-07
    - The dashboard's default live-refresh interval is now 60 seconds
      (was 5). `?refresh=N` still selects any positive interval and
      invalid values fall back to the new default. With 0.49's
      repaint-only-on-change cycle the board still updates in place;
      it now costs one comparison per minute instead of one every five
      seconds.

0.49  2026-08-07
    - Dialog live refresh repaints only on real change (owner video,
      message 2969): the cycle fetches the record, compares it against
      the rendered snapshot, and does nothing when identical — no
      rebuild, no linked-row refetches, no flicker. The editing guard
      now re-checks after the fetch returns, so a refresh response
      already in flight when an editor opens can never evict it.

0.48  2026-08-07
    - CA21 (owner messages 2951/2953): the dialog's Linkage section
